{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01B1sR2apMfQ1K5espCgrurV","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t// Package config persists local TUI preferences across sessions: which\n2\t// editor to open brief.md in, how many entries the dashboard's recent-activity\n3\t// strip may show, and which subscription profile (a bundle of agent CLI +\n4\t// credentials + model) launch.Agent starts a session under.\n5\t//\n6\t// The editor lives in config/tui-settings.json in the manigot checkout. The\n7\t// profile lives in manigot/.env as MANIGOT_PROFILE — the value bare `mg` runs\n8\t// and `mg profiles` write, so CLI and TUI share one profile default. The settings screen (see ui/settings.go)\n9\t// is the only writer; every other reader treats a missing or unreadable file\n10\t// as \"use the defaults\" rather than an error.\n11\tpackage config\n12\t\n13\timport (\n14\t\t\"encoding/json\"\n15\t\t\"fmt\"\n16\t\t\"os\"\n17\t\t\"path/filepath\"\n18\t\t\"strings\"\n19\t\n20\t\t\"github.com/lmuskalla/manigot/internal/home\"\n21\t)\n22\t\n23\t// ToolClaudeCode and ToolOpenCode are the legacy tool values once held by\n24\t// Settings.Tool (and still accepted by the session launcher's --tool alias).\n25\t// New code should use Profiles instead.\n26\tconst (\n27\t\tToolClaudeCode = \"claude-code\"\n28\t\tToolOpenCode   = \"opencode\"\n29\t)\n30\t\n31\t// ProfileClaudePro, ProfileZAI, ProfileOpenCodeGo, ProfileOpenCodeZen and\n32\t// ProfileOpenCodeZenFree are the five supported subscription profiles:\n33\t// Settings.Profile holds one of them, and the session launcher accepts them\n34\t// as --profile values.\n35\tconst (\n36\t\tProfileClaudePro       = \"claude-pro\"\n37\t\tProfileZAI             = \"zai\"\n38\t\tProfileOpenCodeGo      = \"opencode-go\"\n39\t\tProfileOpenCodeZen     = \"opencode-zen\"\n40\t\tProfileOpenCodeZenFree = \"opencode-zen-free\"\n41\t)\n42\t\n43\t// DefaultRecentActivityCount is the maximum number of entries the dashboard's\n44\t// recent-activity strip may show when Settings.RecentActivityCount is unset\n45\t// (zero) — the pre-existing fixed ceiling of 5.\n46\tconst DefaultRecentActivityCount = 5\n47\t\n48\t// Profile describes one of the subscription profiles manigot can run a session\n49\t// under. Each profile bundles the agent CLI to launch, the credentials it is\n50\t// billed against, and the model the CLI should default to — the whole point is\n51\t// that switching profile, not tool, is what changes which subscription is used.\n52\t//\n53\t// The field set is the complete definition of a profile: the five built-in\n54\t// subscription profiles populate every field, and a user-defined profile (see\n55\t// AddProfile) carries the same full definition so the session launcher, the\n56\t// mg profiles listing, and the mg setup wizard all treat it identically to a\n57\t// built-in. The json tags drive the config/profiles.json store; the built-in\n58\t// table is compiled in, never serialized.\n59\ttype Profile struct {\n60\t\t// ID is the value Settings.Profile (and the session launcher's\n61\t\t// --profile flag) holds: ProfileClaudePro, ProfileZAI, ProfileOpenCodeGo,\n62\t\t// ProfileOpenCodeZen, ProfileOpenCodeZenFree, or a user-defined id.\n63\t\tID string `json:\"id\"`\n64\t\n65\t\t// Label is a short human name for the settings screen and help text.\n66\t\tLabel string `json:\"label\"`\n67\t\n68\t\t// Tool is the agent CLI this profile launches: ToolClaudeCode or\n69\t\t// ToolOpenCode.\n70\t\tTool string `json:\"tool\"`\n71\t\n72\t\t// Auth names the credential the profile is billed against, for display in\n73\t\t// the settings screen and setup help.\n74\t\tAuth string `json:\"auth\"`\n75\t\n76\t\t// AuthKeys lists the .env keys the profile needs to be ready, in the\n77\t\t// order the old scripts checked them: claude-pro needs all four\n78\t\t// subscription keys, the opencode profiles their single auth key. The\n79\t\t// session launcher validates and forwards exactly these keys.\n80\t\tAuthKeys []string `json:\"authKeys,omitempty\"`\n81\t\n82\t\t// ModelEnv is the .env key that overrides the profile's default model\n83\t\t// (e.g. OPENCODE_ZAI_MODEL, or a claude-code profile's own model env key).\n84\t\t// Empty means the profile has no override key — claude-pro has none, so\n85\t\t// its ModelEnv is \"\".\n86\t\tModelEnv string `json:\"modelEnv,omitempty\"`\n87\t\n88\t\t// ModelDefault is the model string used when no ModelEnv override is set,\n89\t\t// forwarded to the CLI as a real --model value for both opencode and\n90\t\t// claude-code profiles (session.ResolveProfile's envDefault(ModelEnv,\n91\t\t// ModelDefault)). Empty means \"no override — let the CLI use its own\n92\t\t// default\", which is claude-pro's shape: it defines neither field, so its\n93\t\t// resolved model is always \"\" and nothing is forwarded. (The display-only\n94\t\t// \"(Claude Code default)\" placeholder shown in `mg profiles`' table lives\n95\t\t// in cmd/mg/profiles.go's profileModel(), not in this field.)\n96\t\tModelDefault string `json:\"modelDefault,omitempty\"`\n97\t}\n98\t\n99\t// builtInProfiles is the ordered list of the built-in subscription profiles,\n100\t// in the order the TUI settings screen cycles them. It is the single source\n101\t// of the per-profile auth/model metadata that used to live scattered across\n102\t// cmd/mg/profiles.go (profileAuthKeys/profileModelEnv/profileModelDefaults) and\n103\t// the hardcoded switch in internal/session. The opencode profiles' effective\n104\t// model comes from the OPENCODE_ZAI_MODEL/OPENCODE_GO_MODEL/OPENCODE_ZEN_MODEL/\n105\t// OPENCODE_ZEN_FREE_MODEL values in manigot's .env, falling back to these\n106\t// ModelDefault strings.\n107\tvar builtInProfiles = []Profile{\n108\t\t{\n109\t\t\tID:       ProfileClaudePro,\n110\t\t\tLabel:    \"Claude Code · Claude Pro\",\n111\t\t\tTool:     ToolClaudeCode,\n112\t\t\tAuth:     \"Claude Pro OAuth\",\n113\t\t\tAuthKeys: []string{\"CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN\", \"CLAUDE_ACCOUNT_UUID\", \"CLAUDE_EMAIL\", \"CLAUDE_ORG_UUID\"},\n114\t\t\t// No ModelEnv/ModelDefault — claude-pro lets the CLI use its own\n115\t\t\t// default; see the Profile.ModelDefault doc comment for why this must\n116\t\t\t// not hold a display sentinel now that claude-tool profiles forward\n117\t\t\t// ModelDefault as a real --model value.\n118\t\t},\n119\t\t{\n120\t\t\tID:           ProfileZAI,\n121\t\t\tLabel:        \"OpenCode · Z.AI Coding Plan\",\n122\t\t\tTool:         ToolOpenCode,\n123\t\t\tAuth:         \"ZHIPU_API_KEY\",\n124\t\t\tAuthKeys:     []string{\"ZHIPU_API_KEY\"},\n125\t\t\tModelEnv:     \"OPENCODE_ZAI_MODEL\",\n126\t\t\tModelDefault: \"zai-coding-plan/glm-5.2\",\n127\t\t},\n128\t\t{\n129\t\t\tID:           ProfileOpenCodeGo,\n130\t\t\tLabel:        \"OpenCode · Go\",\n131\t\t\tTool:         ToolOpenCode,\n132\t\t\tAuth:         \"OPENCODE_API_KEY\",\n133\t\t\tAuthKeys:     []string{\"OPENCODE_API_KEY\"},\n134\t\t\tModelEnv:     \"OPENCODE_GO_MODEL\",\n135\t\t\tModelDefault: \"opencode-go/glm-5.2\",\n136\t\t},\n137\t\t{\n138\t\t\tID:           ProfileOpenCodeZen,\n139\t\t\tLabel:        \"OpenCode · Zen\",\n140\t\t\tTool:         ToolOpenCode,\n141\t\t\tAuth:         \"OPENCODE_API_KEY\",\n142\t\t\tAuthKeys:     []string{\"OPENCODE_API_KEY\"},\n143\t\t\tModelEnv:     \"OPENCODE_ZEN_MODEL\",\n144\t\t\tModelDefault: \"opencode/deepseek-v4-flash\",\n145\t\t},\n146\t\t{\n147\t\t\tID:           ProfileOpenCodeZenFree,\n148\t\t\tLabel:        \"OpenCode · Zen Free\",\n149\t\t\tTool:         ToolOpenCode,\n150\t\t\tAuth:         \"OPENCODE_API_KEY\",\n151\t\t\tAuthKeys:     []string{\"OPENCODE_API_KEY\"},\n152\t\t\tModelEnv:     \"OPENCODE_ZEN_FREE_MODEL\",\n153\t\t\tModelDefault: \"opencode/deepseek-v4-flash-free\",\n154\t\t},\n155\t}\n156\t\n157\t// Profiles returns the supported subscription profiles: the built-in table in\n158\t// its canonical order (which drives the TUI cycle and the mg profiles listing),\n159\t// then any user-defined profiles in file order. A missing or corrupt user store\n160\t// degrades to the built-ins only. The slice is built fresh so a caller cannot\n161\t// mutate the shared table.\n162\tfunc Profiles() []Profile {\n163\t\tps := append([]Profile(nil), builtInProfiles...)\n164\t\tfor _, p := range loadUserProfiles() {\n165\t\t\tps = append(ps, p)\n166\t\t}\n167\t\treturn ps\n168\t}\n169\t\n170\t// ProfileByID returns the profile with the given ID, searching the built-in\n171\t// table first then any user-defined profiles. A missing or corrupt user store\n172\t// degrades to the built-ins only (no error — a bad profiles.json must never\n173\t// break a lookup that the read-only CLI listing, TUI, or session launcher\n174\t// depends on).\n175\tfunc ProfileByID(id string) (Profile, bool) {\n176\t\tfor _, p := range builtInProfiles {\n177\t\t\tif p.ID == id {\n178\t\t\t\treturn p, true\n179\t\t\t}\n180\t\t}\n181\t\tfor _, p := range loadUserProfiles() {\n182\t\t\tif p.ID == id {\n183\t\t\t\treturn p, true\n184\t\t\t}\n185\t\t}\n186\t\treturn Profile{}, false\n187\t}\n188\t\n189\t// profilesPath returns the user-profile store's path (config/profiles.json in\n190\t// the manigot checkout), or \"\" if the checkout cannot be located. The file is\n191\t// machine-specific user state, alongside config/tui-settings.json — both are\n192\t// covered by the /config/ ignore rule and never committed.\n193\tfunc profilesPath() string {\n194\t\tdir := Dir()\n195\t\tif dir == \"\" {\n196\t\t\treturn \"\"\n197\t\t}\n198\t\treturn filepath.Join(dir, \"profiles.json\")\n199\t}\n200\t\n201\t// loadUserProfiles returns the user-defined profiles persisted in\n202\t// config/profiles.json, in file order. A missing or corrupt file is not an\n203\t// error — it degrades to an empty set (\"built-ins only\"), so the read-only\n204\t// paths (Profiles/ProfileByID/TUI) can never be broken by a bad store. It is\n205\t// the error-tolerating wrapper around loadUserProfilesErr for those paths.\n206\tfunc loadUserProfiles() []Profile {\n207\t\tps, _ := loadUserProfilesErr()\n208\t\treturn ps\n209\t}\n210\t\n211\t// loadUserProfilesErr is loadUserProfiles' strict form, used by the write\n212\t// paths (AddProfile/RemoveProfile): it reports a corrupt or unreadable file as\n213\t// an error so the caller does not silently overwrite user state.\n214\tfunc loadUserProfilesErr() ([]Profile, error) {\n215\t\tpath := profilesPath()\n216\t\tif path == \"\" {\n217\t\t\treturn nil, nil\n218\t\t}\n219\t\tdata, err := os.ReadFile(path)\n220\t\tif err != nil {\n221\t\t\tif os.IsNotExist(err) {\n222\t\t\t\treturn nil, nil\n223\t\t\t}\n224\t\t\treturn nil, err\n225\t\t}\n226\t\tvar ps []Profile\n227\t\tif err := json.Unmarshal(data, &ps); err != nil {\n228\t\t\treturn nil, fmt.Errorf(\"%s: %w\", path, err)\n229\t\t}\n230\t\treturn ps, nil\n231\t}\n232\t\n233\t// saveUserProfiles writes the user-defined profiles to config/profiles.json,\n234\t// creating config/ (and the file) as needed.\n235\tfunc saveUserProfiles(ps []Profile) error {\n236\t\tdir := Dir()\n237\t\tif dir == \"\" {\n238\t\t\treturn fmt.Errorf(\"cannot determine the manigot checkout to save profiles into (set $%s)\", home.EnvHome)\n239\t\t}\n240\t\tif err := os.MkdirAll(dir, 0o755); err != nil {\n241\t\t\treturn err\n242\t\t}\n243\t\tdata, err := json.MarshalIndent(ps, \"\", \"  \")\n244\t\tif err != nil {\n245\t\t\treturn err\n246\t\t}\n247\t\treturn os.WriteFile(filepath.Join(dir, \"profiles.json\"), data, 0o644)\n248\t}\n249\t\n250\t// AddProfile persists a user-defined profile, appending it after the built-ins\n251\t// and any existing user profiles (file order). The profile id must be unique\n252\t// across the whole set — a collision with a built-in or an existing user\n253\t// profile is rejected. Built-ins are never modified; AddProfile only ever\n254\t// writes the user store.\n255\tfunc AddProfile(p Profile) error {\n256\t\tif strings.TrimSpace(p.ID) == \"\" {\n257\t\t\treturn fmt.Errorf(\"a profile id is required\")\n258\t\t}\n259\t\tfor _, b := range builtInProfiles {\n260\t\t\tif b.ID == p.ID {\n261\t\t\t\treturn fmt.Errorf(\"a built-in profile with id %q already exists\", p.ID)\n262\t\t\t}\n263\t\t}\n264\t\tps, err := loadUserProfilesErr()\n265\t\tif err != nil {\n266\t\t\treturn err\n267\t\t}\n268\t\tfor _, existing := range ps {\n269\t\t\tif existing.ID == p.ID {\n270\t\t\t\treturn fmt.Errorf(\"a profile with id %q already exists\", p.ID)\n271\t\t\t}\n272\t\t}\n273\t\tps = append(ps, p)\n274\t\treturn saveUserProfiles(ps)\n275\t}\n276\t\n277\t// RemoveProfile deletes a user-defined profile by id. Built-in profiles are\n278\t// not deletable — they are the defaults every user starts from; only\n279\t// user-defined ids can be removed. Removing an id that is not in the user\n280\t// store is an error.\n281\tfunc RemoveProfile(id string) error {\n282\t\tps, err := loadUserProfilesErr()\n283\t\tif err != nil {\n284\t\t\treturn err\n285\t\t}\n286\t\tkept := ps[:0]\n287\t\tfound := false\n288\t\tfor _, p := range ps {\n289\t\t\tif p.ID == id {\n290\t\t\t\tfound = true\n291\t\t\t\tcontinue\n292\t\t\t}\n293\t\t\tkept = append(kept, p)\n294\t\t}\n295\t\tif !found {\n296\t\t\treturn fmt.Errorf(\"no user-defined profile with id %q\", id)\n297\t\t}\n298\t\treturn saveUserProfiles(kept)\n299\t}\n300\t\n301\t// ProfileTool returns the agent CLI a profile runs, defaulting to\n302\t// ToolClaudeCode for an unrecognized ID.\n303\tfunc ProfileTool(id string) string {\n304\t\tif p, ok := ProfileByID(id); ok {\n305\t\t\treturn p.Tool\n306\t\t}\n307\t\treturn ToolClaudeCode\n308\t}\n309\t\n310\t// Settings holds the TUI's persisted preferences.\n311\ttype Settings struct {\n312\t\t// Editor is the command run to open brief.md (the detail view's \"e\"\n313\t\t// shortcut), e.g. \"vim\" or \"nano\". Empty means fall back to\n314\t\t// $VISUAL/$EDITOR/nano/vi — see editor.Resolve.\n315\t\tEditor string `json:\"editor\"`\n316\t\n317\t\t// Profile selects the subscription profile launch.Agent/Quick use:\n318\t\t// ProfileClaudePro, ProfileZAI, ProfileOpenCodeGo, ProfileOpenCodeZen or\n319\t\t// ProfileOpenCodeZenFree. It is the shared\n320\t\t// default — loaded from MANIGOT_PROFILE in manigot/.env (with the legacy\n321\t\t// profile/tool fields of tui-settings.json as migration fallbacks) and\n322\t\t// persisted to .env by Save, never to tui-settings.json. Empty is treated\n323\t\t// as ProfileClaudePro — see ProfileValue.\n324\t\tProfile string `json:\"profile,omitempty\"`\n325\t\n326\t\t// RecentActivityCount is the maximum number of entries the dashboard's\n327\t\t// recent-activity strip may show (git.RecentCommits' fetch count and the\n328\t\t// clamp upper bound in recentActivityShown). 0 (unset — the JSON zero\n329\t\t// value) means DefaultRecentActivityCount — see RecentActivityCountValue.\n330\t\t// Valid user-set range is 1–100, enforced by the settings form.\n331\t\tRecentActivityCount int `json:\"recentActivityCount\"`\n332\t\n333\t\t// Tool is the legacy pre-profile selector (ToolClaudeCode/ToolOpenCode),\n334\t\t// kept only so old tui-settings.json files load. It is migrated into\n335\t\t// Profile by Load and never written back.\n336\t\tTool string `json:\"tool,omitempty\"`\n337\t\n338\t\t// Terminal is the command used to spawn an agent session's terminal\n339\t\t// (launch.Agent/Quick/AgentQuick), e.g. \"kitty\" or \"alacritty -e\". Empty\n340\t\t// means fall back to auto-detect (macOS Terminal.app, then a Linux\n341\t\t// emulator list). When set, it applies only when the TUI is not inside\n342\t\t// tmux: inside tmux the launch is always a tmux split pane, regardless of\n343\t\t// this setting — see internal/launch's buildCmd.\n344\t\tTerminal string `json:\"terminal,omitempty\"`\n345\t\n346\t\t// Theme is the global OpenCode theme name (e.g. \"nord\", \"tokyonight\") —\n347\t\t// one value shared across every OpenCode profile, unlike the per-profile\n348\t\t// OPENCODE_*_MODEL keys. It is the shared default: loaded from\n349\t\t// OPENCODE_THEME in manigot/.env and persisted there by Save, never to\n350\t\t// tui-settings.json — mirroring Profile's storage exactly. Empty means\n351\t\t// \"let OpenCode use its own default/config\" — see ThemeValue. Unlike\n352\t\t// Profile, an unrecognized value is still accepted: OpenCode's theme list\n353\t\t// is not validated here (see `mg theme`).\n354\t\tTheme string `json:\"-\"`\n355\t}\n356\t\n357\t// ProfileValue returns s.Profile, defaulting to ProfileClaudePro when unset.\n358\tfunc (s Settings) ProfileValue() string {\n359\t\tif s.Profile == \"\" {\n360\t\t\treturn ProfileClaudePro\n361\t\t}\n362\t\treturn s.Profile\n363\t}\n364\t\n365\t// ThemeValue returns s.Theme, defaulting to \"\" (meaning \"let OpenCode use its\n366\t// own default/config\") when unset. Unlike ProfileValue, there is no non-empty\n367\t// fallback — an empty theme is a legitimate, common choice, not an invalid\n368\t// one.\n369\tfunc (s Settings) ThemeValue() string {\n370\t\treturn s.Theme\n371\t}\n372\t\n373\t// RecentActivityCountValue returns s.RecentActivityCount, defaulting to\n374\t// DefaultRecentActivityCount when unset (≤ 0, the JSON zero value).\n375\tfunc (s Settings) RecentActivityCountValue() int {\n376\t\tif s.RecentActivityCount <= 0 {\n377\t\t\treturn DefaultRecentActivityCount\n378\t\t}\n379\t\treturn s.RecentActivityCount\n380\t}\n381\t\n382\t// EnvFile returns the manigot checkout's .env file path, or \"\" if Dir does.\n383\t// .env is the shared store for the default profile: MANIGOT_PROFILE there is\n384\t// read by bare `mg` runs, written by `mg profiles`, and read/written here so\n385\t// the TUI and CLI share one profile default.\n386\tfunc EnvFile() string {\n387\t\thome := home.Root()\n388\t\tif home == \"\" {\n389\t\t\treturn \"\"\n390\t\t}\n391\t\treturn filepath.Join(home, \".env\")\n392\t}\n393\t\n394\t// GetEnv returns the value of key in the manigot checkout's .env (\"\" when\n395\t// absent or unreadable). Lines may carry an `export ` prefix or surrounding\n396\t// quotes — both are tolerated, mirroring the leniency of sourcing a shell\n397\t// env file. It is the generic form of the profile lookup that\n398\t// readEnvProfile wraps; the ported CLI commands (mg profiles, mg setup) use it\n399\t// to read any credential key.\n400\tfunc GetEnv(key string) string {\n401\t\tv, _ := envValue(key)\n402\t\treturn v\n403\t}\n404\t\n405\t// envValue returns the value of key in the manigot checkout's .env, reporting\n406\t// whether the key was present at all (an explicit \"KEY=\" counts as\n407\t// present-but-empty, which matters once the file is sourced: sourcing\n408\t// overwrites an inherited value with the empty one).\n409\tfunc envValue(key string) (string, bool) {\n410\t\tdata, err := os.ReadFile(EnvFile())\n411\t\tif err != nil {\n412\t\t\treturn \"\", false\n413\t\t}\n414\t\tfor _, line := range strings.Split(string(data), \"\\n\") {\n415\t\t\tline = strings.TrimSpace(line)\n416\t\t\tif line == \"\" || strings.HasPrefix(line, \"#\") {\n417\t\t\t\tcontinue\n418\t\t\t}\n419\t\t\tline = strings.TrimPrefix(line, \"export \")\n420\t\t\tk, value, ok := strings.Cut(line, \"=\")\n421\t\t\tif !ok || strings.TrimSpace(k) != key {\n422\t\t\t\tcontinue\n423\t\t\t}\n424\t\t\treturn unquoteEnvValue(strings.TrimSpace(value)), true\n425\t\t}\n426\t\treturn \"\", false\n427\t}\n428\t\n429\t// EnvValue returns the effective value of key the way the old scripts saw it:\n430\t// the .env value when the key is present there (sourcing exports it into the\n431\t// process environment, overwriting anything inherited), else the process\n432\t// environment. The ported CLI commands use it for readiness checks so an\n433\t// inherited export counts as configured, exactly like the scripts' `${!KEY:-}`\n434\t// reads did.\n435\tfunc EnvValue(key string) string {\n436\t\tif v, ok := envValue(key); ok {\n437\t\t\treturn v\n438\t\t}\n439\t\treturn os.Getenv(key)\n440\t}\n441\t\n442\t// unquoteEnvValue strips one pair of surrounding double or single quotes from\n443\t// an .env value, mirroring how the shell would source it.\n444\tfunc unquoteEnvValue(value string) string {\n445\t\tif len(value) >= 2 {\n446\t\t\tif (value[0] == '\"' && value[len(value)-1] == '\"') ||\n447\t\t\t\t(value[0] == '\\'' && value[len(value)-1] == '\\'') {\n448\t\t\t\treturn value[1 : len(value)-1]\n449\t\t\t}\n450\t\t}\n451\t\treturn value\n452\t}\n453\t\n454\t// UpsertEnv upserts key=value into the manigot checkout's .env, preserving\n455\t// every other line (credentials, comments, model overrides). The file is\n456\t// created with the standard header when it does not exist yet. value is\n457\t// written unquoted, matching the format config's own reader tolerates.\n458\t// It is the generic form of writeEnvProfile; the ported CLI commands use it to\n459\t// write any credential or model key.\n460\tfunc UpsertEnv(key, value string) error {\n461\t\tpath := EnvFile()\n462\t\tif path == \"\" {\n463\t\t\treturn fmt.Errorf(\"cannot determine the manigot checkout to write %s into (set $%s)\", key, home.EnvHome)\n464\t\t}\n465\t\n466\t\tcontent, err := os.ReadFile(path)\n467\t\tvar lines []string\n468\t\tswitch {\n469\t\tcase err == nil:\n470\t\t\tlines = strings.Split(string(content), \"\\n\")\n471\t\tcase os.IsNotExist(err):\n472\t\t\t// Seed a fresh file with the same header scripts/profiles.sh and\n473\t\t\t// scripts/setup.sh write.\n474\t\t\tlines = []string{\"# manigot configuration — credentials and defaults (never commit this file)\"}\n475\t\tdefault:\n476\t\t\treturn err\n477\t\t}\n478\t\n479\t\treplaced := false\n480\t\tfor i, line := range lines {\n481\t\t\ttrimmed := strings.TrimSpace(line)\n482\t\t\tif trimmed == \"\" || strings.HasPrefix(trimmed, \"#\") {\n483\t\t\t\tcontinue\n484\t\t\t}\n485\t\t\tif k, _, ok := strings.Cut(strings.TrimPrefix(trimmed, \"export \"), \"=\"); ok && strings.TrimSpace(k) == key {\n486\t\t\t\tlines[i] = key + \"=\" + value\n487\t\t\t\treplaced = true\n488\t\t\t\tbreak\n489\t\t\t}\n490\t\t}\n491\t\tif !replaced {\n492\t\t\tif last := lines[len(lines)-1]; last == \"\" {\n493\t\t\t\t// The file ended with a newline — reuse the trailing empty\n494\t\t\t\t// element rather than leaving a blank line above the append.\n495\t\t\t\tlines[len(lines)-1] = key + \"=\" + value\n496\t\t\t} else {\n497\t\t\t\t// No trailing newline — add one so the new line starts fresh.\n498\t\t\t\tlines = append(lines, \"\", key+\"=\"+value)\n499\t\t\t}\n500\t\t}\n501\t\n502\t\treturn os.WriteFile(path, []byte(strings.Join(lines, \"\\n\")), 0o644)\n503\t}\n504\t\n505\t// readEnvProfile returns the MANIGOT_PROFILE value from manigot/.env (\"\" when\n506\t// absent or unreadable). It is a best-effort scan tolerant of shell-isms in\n507\t// the file: a missing .env is the normal pre-first\n508\t// save state and the caller falls back to the legacy tui-settings.json fields\n509\t// and finally to ProfileClaudePro. Lines may carry an `export ` prefix or\n510\t// surrounding quotes — both are tolerated.\n511\tfunc readEnvProfile() string {\n512\t\treturn GetEnv(\"MANIGOT_PROFILE\")\n513\t}\n514\t\n515\t// writeEnvProfile upserts MANIGOT_PROFILE=<profile> into manigot/.env,\n516\t// preserving every other line (credentials, comments, model overrides). The\n517\t// file is created with the standard header when it does not exist yet.\n518\t// profile must already be validated by the caller (see Save); it is written\n519\t// unquoted, matching the format config's own reader tolerates.\n520\tfunc writeEnvProfile(profile string) error {\n521\t\treturn UpsertEnv(\"MANIGOT_PROFILE\", profile)\n522\t}\n523\t\n524\t// Dir returns the directory the settings file lives in: config/ inside the\n525\t// manigot checkout the running binary belongs to (see home.Root). It is\n526\t// \"\" if that checkout could not be located.\n527\tfunc Dir() string {\n528\t\thome := home.Root()\n529\t\tif home == \"\" {\n530\t\t\treturn \"\"\n531\t\t}\n532\t\treturn filepath.Join(home, \"config\")\n533\t}\n534\t\n535\t// Path returns the settings file's path, or \"\" if Dir does.\n536\tfunc Path() string {\n537\t\tdir := Dir()\n538\t\tif dir == \"\" {\n539\t\t\treturn \"\"\n540\t\t}\n541\t\treturn filepath.Join(dir, \"tui-settings.json\")\n542\t}\n543\t\n544\t// Load reads the settings. A file that does not exist yet (nothing has been\n545\t// saved, or the checkout could not be located) is not an error — it yields the\n546\t// zero-value Settings, which every caller treats as \"defaults\".\n547\t//\n548\t// Profile resolution order: MANIGOT_PROFILE in manigot/.env — the shared,\n549\t// central store, written by both `mg profiles` and the TUI's settings screen —\n550\t// wins when present and valid. Failing that, the legacy `profile` field of\n551\t// tui-settings.json (and the even older `tool` field, migrated:\n552\t// \"claude-code\" maps to ProfileClaudePro, \"opencode\" maps to ProfileZAI) is\n553\t// honored as a migration fallback so an existing user's choice survives the\n554\t// upgrade. Neither legacy field is written back — Save persists the profile to\n555\t// .env only.\n556\tfunc Load() (Settings, error) {\n557\t\tpath := Path()\n558\t\tvar s Settings\n559\t\tif path != \"\" {\n560\t\t\tdata, err := os.ReadFile(path)\n561\t\t\tswitch {\n562\t\t\tcase err == nil:\n563\t\t\t\tif err := json.Unmarshal(data, &s); err != nil {\n564\t\t\t\t\treturn Settings{}, fmt.Errorf(\"%s: %w\", path, err)\n565\t\t\t\t}\n566\t\t\tcase os.IsNotExist(err):\n567\t\t\t\t// nothing saved yet — zero value, defaults apply\n568\t\t\tdefault:\n569\t\t\t\treturn Settings{}, err\n570\t\t\t}\n571\t\t}\n572\t\n573\t\tif envProfile := readEnvProfile(); envProfile != \"\" {\n574\t\t\tif _, ok := ProfileByID(envProfile); ok {\n575\t\t\t\ts.Profile = envProfile\n576\t\t\t}\n577\t\t}\n578\t\tif s.Profile == \"\" && s.Tool != \"\" {\n579\t\t\tswitch s.Tool {\n580\t\t\tcase ToolOpenCode:\n581\t\t\t\ts.Profile = ProfileZAI\n582\t\t\tdefault:\n583\t\t\t\ts.Profile = ProfileClaudePro\n584\t\t\t}\n585\t\t}\n586\t\ts.Tool = \"\" // legacy field — not written back\n587\t\ts.Theme = GetEnv(\"OPENCODE_THEME\")\n588\t\treturn s, nil\n589\t}\n590\t\n591\t// Save writes s to disk: the editor to config/tui-settings.json (creating\n592\t// config/ if needed) and the profile to manigot/.env as MANIGOT_PROFILE —\n593\t// the shared default bare `mg` and the TUI both use. The profile is no longer\n594\t// written to tui-settings.json: that field (like the older `tool` field) is\n595\t// legacy, read for migration by Load only.\n596\tfunc Save(s Settings) error {\n597\t\tdir := Dir()\n598\t\tif dir == \"\" {\n599\t\t\treturn fmt.Errorf(\"cannot determine the manigot checkout to save settings into (set $%s)\", home.EnvHome)\n600\t\t}\n601\t\tif err := os.MkdirAll(dir, 0o755); err != nil {\n602\t\t\treturn err\n603\t\t}\n604\t\tlegacy := s\n605\t\tlegacy.Profile = \"\" // not persisted here anymore — `omitempty` drops it\n606\t\tdata, err := json.MarshalIndent(legacy, \"\", \"  \")\n607\t\tif err != nil {\n608\t\t\treturn err\n609\t\t}\n610\t\tif err := os.WriteFile(filepath.Join(dir, \"tui-settings.json\"), data, 0o644); err != nil {\n611\t\t\treturn err\n612\t\t}\n613\t\n614\t\tprofile := s.ProfileValue()\n615\t\tif _, ok := ProfileByID(profile); !ok {\n616\t\t\treturn fmt.Errorf(\"cannot save: %q is not a valid profile id\", profile)\n617\t\t}\n618\t\tif err := writeEnvProfile(profile); err != nil {\n619\t\t\treturn err\n620\t\t}\n621\t\treturn UpsertEnv(\"OPENCODE_THEME\", s.Theme)\n622\t}\n623\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4e2afcf5-7fc0-4a90-a6a6-e82e43a08449","uuid":"7d5d5a4b-6ad1-434d-8047-e8245bb2d82f","timestamp":"2026-08-31T13:46:10.771Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/workspace/src/internal/config/config.go","content":"// Package config persists local TUI preferences across sessions: which\n// editor to open brief.md in, how many entries the dashboard's recent-activity\n// strip may show, and which subscription profile (a bundle of agent CLI +\n// credentials + model) launch.Agent starts a session under.\n//\n// The editor lives in config/tui-settings.json in the manigot checkout. The\n// profile lives in manigot/.env as MANIGOT_PROFILE — the value bare `mg` runs\n// and `mg profiles` write, so CLI and TUI share one profile default. The settings screen (see ui/settings.go)\n// is the only writer; every other reader treats a missing or unreadable file\n// as \"use the defaults\" rather than an error.\npackage config\n\nimport (\n\t\"encoding/json\"\n\t\"fmt\"\n\t\"os\"\n\t\"path/filepath\"\n\t\"strings\"\n\n\t\"github.com/lmuskalla/manigot/internal/home\"\n)\n\n// ToolClaudeCode and ToolOpenCode are the legacy tool values once held by\n// Settings.Tool (and still accepted by the session launcher's --tool alias).\n// New code should use Profiles instead.\nconst (\n\tToolClaudeCode = \"claude-code\"\n\tToolOpenCode   = \"opencode\"\n)\n\n// ProfileClaudePro, ProfileZAI, ProfileOpenCodeGo, ProfileOpenCodeZen and\n// ProfileOpenCodeZenFree are the five supported subscription profiles:\n// Settings.Profile holds one of them, and the session launcher accepts them\n// as --profile values.\nconst (\n\tProfileClaudePro       = \"claude-pro\"\n\tProfileZAI             = \"zai\"\n\tProfileOpenCodeGo      = \"opencode-go\"\n\tProfileOpenCodeZen     = \"opencode-zen\"\n\tProfileOpenCodeZenFree = \"opencode-zen-free\"\n)\n\n// DefaultRecentActivityCount is the maximum number of entries the dashboard's\n// recent-activity strip may show when Settings.RecentActivityCount is unset\n// (zero) — the pre-existing fixed ceiling of 5.\nconst DefaultRecentActivityCount = 5\n\n// Profile describes one of the subscription profiles manigot can run a session\n// under. Each profile bundles the agent CLI to launch, the credentials it is\n// billed against, and the model the CLI should default to — the whole point is\n// that switching profile, not tool, is what changes which subscription is used.\n//\n// The field set is the complete definition of a profile: the five built-in\n// subscription profiles populate every field, and a user-defined profile (see\n// AddProfile) carries the same full definition so the session launcher, the\n// mg profiles listing, and the mg setup wizard all treat it identically to a\n// built-in. The json tags drive the config/profiles.json store; the built-in\n// table is compiled in, never serialized.\ntype Profile struct {\n\t// ID is the value Settings.Profile (and the session launcher's\n\t// --profile flag) holds: ProfileClaudePro, ProfileZAI, ProfileOpenCodeGo,\n\t// ProfileOpenCodeZen, ProfileOpenCodeZenFree, or a user-defined id.\n\tID string `json:\"id\"`\n\n\t// Label is a short human name for the settings screen and help text.\n\tLabel string `json:\"label\"`\n\n\t// Tool is the agent CLI this profile launches: ToolClaudeCode or\n\t// ToolOpenCode.\n\tTool string `json:\"tool\"`\n\n\t// Auth names the credential the profile is billed against, for display in\n\t// the settings screen and setup help.\n\tAuth string `json:\"auth\"`\n\n\t// AuthKeys lists the .env keys the profile needs to be ready, in the\n\t// order the old scripts checked them: claude-pro needs all four\n\t// subscription keys, the opencode profiles their single auth key. The\n\t// session launcher validates and forwards exactly these keys.\n\tAuthKeys []string `json:\"authKeys,omitempty\"`\n\n\t// ModelEnv is the .env key that overrides the profile's default model\n\t// (e.g. OPENCODE_ZAI_MODEL, or a claude-code profile's own model env key).\n\t// Empty means the profile has no override key — claude-pro has none, so\n\t// its ModelEnv is \"\".\n\tModelEnv string `json:\"modelEnv,omitempty\"`\n\n\t// ModelDefault is the model string used when no ModelEnv override is set,\n\t// forwarded to the CLI as a real --model value for both opencode and\n\t// claude-code profiles (session.ResolveProfile's envDefault(ModelEnv,\n\t// ModelDefault)). Empty means \"no override — let the CLI use its own\n\t// default\", which is claude-pro's shape: it defines neither field, so its\n\t// resolved model is always \"\" and nothing is forwarded. (The display-only\n\t// \"(Claude Code default)\" placeholder shown in `mg profiles`' table lives\n\t// in cmd/mg/profiles.go's profileModel(), not in this field.)\n\tModelDefault string `json:\"modelDefault,omitempty\"`\n}\n\n// builtInProfiles is the ordered list of the built-in subscription profiles,\n// in the order the TUI settings screen cycles them. It is the single source\n// of the per-profile auth/model metadata that used to live scattered across\n// cmd/mg/profiles.go (profileAuthKeys/profileModelEnv/profileModelDefaults) and\n// the hardcoded switch in internal/session. The opencode profiles' effective\n// model comes from the OPENCODE_ZAI_MODEL/OPENCODE_GO_MODEL/OPENCODE_ZEN_MODEL/\n// OPENCODE_ZEN_FREE_MODEL values in manigot's .env, falling back to these\n// ModelDefault strings.\nvar builtInProfiles = []Profile{\n\t{\n\t\tID:       ProfileClaudePro,\n\t\tLabel:    \"Claude Code · Claude Pro\",\n\t\tTool:     ToolClaudeCode,\n\t\tAuth:     \"Claude Pro OAuth\",\n\t\tAuthKeys: []string{\"CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN\", \"CLAUDE_ACCOUNT_UUID\", \"CLAUDE_EMAIL\", \"CLAUDE_ORG_UUID\"},\n\t\t// No ModelEnv/ModelDefault — claude-pro lets the CLI use its own\n\t\t// default; see the Profile.ModelDefault doc comment for why this must\n\t\t// not hold a display sentinel now that claude-tool profiles forward\n\t\t// ModelDefault as a real --model value.\n\t},\n\t{\n\t\tID:           ProfileZAI,\n\t\tLabel:        \"OpenCode · Z.AI Coding Plan\",\n\t\tTool:         ToolOpenCode,\n\t\tAuth:         \"ZHIPU_API_KEY\",\n\t\tAuthKeys:     []string{\"ZHIPU_API_KEY\"},\n\t\tModelEnv:     \"OPENCODE_ZAI_MODEL\",\n\t\tModelDefault: \"zai-coding-plan/glm-5.2\",\n\t},\n\t{\n\t\tID:           ProfileOpenCodeGo,\n\t\tLabel:        \"OpenCode · Go\",\n\t\tTool:         ToolOpenCode,\n\t\tAuth:         \"OPENCODE_API_KEY\",\n\t\tAuthKeys:     []string{\"OPENCODE_API_KEY\"},\n\t\tModelEnv:     \"OPENCODE_GO_MODEL\",\n\t\tModelDefault: \"opencode-go/glm-5.2\",\n\t},\n\t{\n\t\tID:           ProfileOpenCodeZen,\n\t\tLabel:        \"OpenCode · Zen\",\n\t\tTool:         ToolOpenCode,\n\t\tAuth:         \"OPENCODE_API_KEY\",\n\t\tAuthKeys:     []string{\"OPENCODE_API_KEY\"},\n\t\tModelEnv:     \"OPENCODE_ZEN_MODEL\",\n\t\tModelDefault: \"opencode/deepseek-v4-flash\",\n\t},\n\t{\n\t\tID:           ProfileOpenCodeZenFree,\n\t\tLabel:        \"OpenCode · Zen Free\",\n\t\tTool:         ToolOpenCode,\n\t\tAuth:         \"OPENCODE_API_KEY\",\n\t\tAuthKeys:     []string{\"OPENCODE_API_KEY\"},\n\t\tModelEnv:     \"OPENCODE_ZEN_FREE_MODEL\",\n\t\tModelDefault: \"opencode/deepseek-v4-flash-free\",\n\t},\n}\n\n// Profiles returns the supported subscription profiles: the built-in table in\n// its canonical order (which drives the TUI cycle and the mg profiles listing),\n// then any user-defined profiles in file order. A missing or corrupt user store\n// degrades to the built-ins only. The slice is built fresh so a caller cannot\n// mutate the shared table.\nfunc Profiles() []Profile {\n\tps := append([]Profile(nil), builtInProfiles...)\n\tfor _, p := range loadUserProfiles() {\n\t\tps = append(ps, p)\n\t}\n\treturn ps\n}\n\n// ProfileByID returns the profile with the given ID, searching the built-in\n// table first then any user-defined profiles. A missing or corrupt user store\n// degrades to the built-ins only (no error — a bad profiles.json must never\n// break a lookup that the read-only CLI listing, TUI, or session launcher\n// depends on).\nfunc ProfileByID(id string) (Profile, bool) {\n\tfor _, p := range builtInProfiles {\n\t\tif p.ID == id {\n\t\t\treturn p, true\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\tfor _, p := range loadUserProfiles() {\n\t\tif p.ID == id {\n\t\t\treturn p, true\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn Profile{}, false\n}\n\n// profilesPath returns the user-profile store's path (config/profiles.json in\n// the manigot checkout), or \"\" if the checkout cannot be located. The file is\n// machine-specific user state, alongside config/tui-settings.json — both are\n// covered by the /config/ ignore rule and never committed.\nfunc profilesPath() string {\n\tdir := Dir()\n\tif dir == \"\" {\n\t\treturn \"\"\n\t}\n\treturn filepath.Join(dir, \"profiles.json\")\n}\n\n// loadUserProfiles returns the user-defined profiles persisted in\n// config/profiles.json, in file order. A missing or corrupt file is not an\n// error — it degrades to an empty set (\"built-ins only\"), so the read-only\n// paths (Profiles/ProfileByID/TUI) can never be broken by a bad store. It is\n// the error-tolerating wrapper around loadUserProfilesErr for those paths.\nfunc loadUserProfiles() []Profile {\n\tps, _ := loadUserProfilesErr()\n\treturn ps\n}\n\n// loadUserProfilesErr is loadUserProfiles' strict form, used by the write\n// paths (AddProfile/RemoveProfile): it reports a corrupt or unreadable file as\n// an error so the caller does not silently overwrite user state.\nfunc loadUserProfilesErr() ([]Profile, error) {\n\tpath := profilesPath()\n\tif path == \"\" {\n\t\treturn nil, nil\n\t}\n\tdata, err := os.ReadFile(path)\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\tif os.IsNotExist(err) {\n\t\t\treturn nil, nil\n\t\t}\n\t\treturn nil, err\n\t}\n\tvar ps []Profile\n\tif err := json.Unmarshal(data, &ps); err != nil {\n\t\treturn nil, fmt.Errorf(\"%s: %w\", path, err)\n\t}\n\treturn ps, nil\n}\n\n// saveUserProfiles writes the user-defined profiles to config/profiles.json,\n// creating config/ (and the file) as needed.\nfunc saveUserProfiles(ps []Profile) error {\n\tdir := Dir()\n\tif dir == \"\" {\n\t\treturn fmt.Errorf(\"cannot determine the manigot checkout to save profiles into (set $%s)\", home.EnvHome)\n\t}\n\tif err := os.MkdirAll(dir, 0o755); err != nil {\n\t\treturn err\n\t}\n\tdata, err := json.MarshalIndent(ps, \"\", \"  \")\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn err\n\t}\n\treturn os.WriteFile(filepath.Join(dir, \"profiles.json\"), data, 0o644)\n}\n\n// AddProfile persists a user-defined profile, appending it after the built-ins\n// and any existing user profiles (file order). The profile id must be unique\n// across the whole set — a collision with a built-in or an existing user\n// profile is rejected. Built-ins are never modified; AddProfile only ever\n// writes the user store.\nfunc AddProfile(p Profile) error {\n\tif strings.TrimSpace(p.ID) == \"\" {\n\t\treturn fmt.Errorf(\"a profile id is required\")\n\t}\n\tfor _, b := range builtInProfiles {\n\t\tif b.ID == p.ID {\n\t\t\treturn fmt.Errorf(\"a built-in profile with id %q already exists\", p.ID)\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\tps, err := loadUserProfilesErr()\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn err\n\t}\n\tfor _, existing := range ps {\n\t\tif existing.ID == p.ID {\n\t\t\treturn fmt.Errorf(\"a profile with id %q already exists\", p.ID)\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\tps = append(ps, p)\n\treturn saveUserProfiles(ps)\n}\n\n// RemoveProfile deletes a user-defined profile by id. Built-in profiles are\n// not deletable — they are the defaults every user starts from; only\n// user-defined ids can be removed. Removing an id that is not in the user\n// store is an error.\nfunc RemoveProfile(id string) error {\n\tps, err := loadUserProfilesErr()\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn err\n\t}\n\tkept := ps[:0]\n\tfound := false\n\tfor _, p := range ps {\n\t\tif p.ID == id {\n\t\t\tfound = true\n\t\t\tcontinue\n\t\t}\n\t\tkept = append(kept, p)\n\t}\n\tif !found {\n\t\treturn fmt.Errorf(\"no user-defined profile with id %q\", id)\n\t}\n\treturn saveUserProfiles(kept)\n}\n\n// ProfileTool returns the agent CLI a profile runs, defaulting to\n// ToolClaudeCode for an unrecognized ID.\nfunc ProfileTool(id string) string {\n\tif p, ok := ProfileByID(id); ok {\n\t\treturn p.Tool\n\t}\n\treturn ToolClaudeCode\n}\n\n// Settings holds the TUI's persisted preferences.\ntype Settings struct {\n\t// Editor is the command run to open brief.md (the detail view's \"e\"\n\t// shortcut), e.g. \"vim\" or \"nano\". Empty means fall back to\n\t// $VISUAL/$EDITOR/nano/vi — see editor.Resolve.\n\tEditor string `json:\"editor\"`\n\n\t// Profile selects the subscription profile launch.Agent/Quick use:\n\t// ProfileClaudePro, ProfileZAI, ProfileOpenCodeGo, ProfileOpenCodeZen or\n\t// ProfileOpenCodeZenFree. It is the shared\n\t// default — loaded from MANIGOT_PROFILE in manigot/.env (with the legacy\n\t// profile/tool fields of tui-settings.json as migration fallbacks) and\n\t// persisted to .env by Save, never to tui-settings.json. Empty is treated\n\t// as ProfileClaudePro — see ProfileValue.\n\tProfile string `json:\"profile,omitempty\"`\n\n\t// RecentActivityCount is the maximum number of entries the dashboard's\n\t// recent-activity strip may show (git.RecentCommits' fetch count and the\n\t// clamp upper bound in recentActivityShown). 0 (unset — the JSON zero\n\t// value) means DefaultRecentActivityCount — see RecentActivityCountValue.\n\t// Valid user-set range is 1–100, enforced by the settings form.\n\tRecentActivityCount int `json:\"recentActivityCount\"`\n\n\t// Tool is the legacy pre-profile selector (ToolClaudeCode/ToolOpenCode),\n\t// kept only so old tui-settings.json files load. It is migrated into\n\t// Profile by Load and never written back.\n\tTool string `json:\"tool,omitempty\"`\n\n\t// Terminal is the command used to spawn an agent session's terminal\n\t// (launch.Agent/Quick/AgentQuick), e.g. \"kitty\" or \"alacritty -e\". Empty\n\t// means fall back to auto-detect (macOS Terminal.app, then a Linux\n\t// emulator list). When set, it applies only when the TUI is not inside\n\t// tmux: inside tmux the launch is always a tmux split pane, regardless of\n\t// this setting — see internal/launch's buildCmd.\n\tTerminal string `json:\"terminal,omitempty\"`\n\n\t// Theme is the global OpenCode theme name (e.g. \"nord\", \"tokyonight\") —\n\t// one value shared across every OpenCode profile, unlike the per-profile\n\t// OPENCODE_*_MODEL keys. It is the shared default: loaded from\n\t// OPENCODE_THEME in manigot/.env and persisted there by Save, never to\n\t// tui-settings.json — mirroring Profile's storage exactly. Empty means\n\t// \"let OpenCode use its own default/config\" — see ThemeValue. Unlike\n\t// Profile, an unrecognized value is still accepted: OpenCode's theme list\n\t// is not validated here (see `mg theme`).\n\tTheme string `json:\"-\"`\n}\n\n// ProfileValue returns s.Profile, defaulting to ProfileClaudePro when unset.\nfunc (s Settings) ProfileValue() string {\n\tif s.Profile == \"\" {\n\t\treturn ProfileClaudePro\n\t}\n\treturn s.Profile\n}\n\n// ThemeValue returns s.Theme, defaulting to \"\" (meaning \"let OpenCode use its\n// own default/config\") when unset. Unlike ProfileValue, there is no non-empty\n// fallback — an empty theme is a legitimate, common choice, not an invalid\n// one.\nfunc (s Settings) ThemeValue() string {\n\treturn s.Theme\n}\n\n// RecentActivityCountValue returns s.RecentActivityCount, defaulting to\n// DefaultRecentActivityCount when unset (≤ 0, the JSON zero value).\nfunc (s Settings) RecentActivityCountValue() int {\n\tif s.RecentActivityCount <= 0 {\n\t\treturn DefaultRecentActivityCount\n\t}\n\treturn s.RecentActivityCount\n}\n\n// EnvFile returns the manigot checkout's .env file path, or \"\" if Dir does.\n// .env is the shared store for the default profile: MANIGOT_PROFILE there is\n// read by bare `mg` runs, written by `mg profiles`, and read/written here so\n// the TUI and CLI share one profile default.\nfunc EnvFile() string {\n\thome := home.Root()\n\tif home == \"\" {\n\t\treturn \"\"\n\t}\n\treturn filepath.Join(home, \".env\")\n}\n\n// GetEnv returns the value of key in the manigot checkout's .env (\"\" when\n// absent or unreadable). Lines may carry an `export ` prefix or surrounding\n// quotes — both are tolerated, mirroring the leniency of sourcing a shell\n// env file. It is the generic form of the profile lookup that\n// readEnvProfile wraps; the ported CLI commands (mg profiles, mg setup) use it\n// to read any credential key.\nfunc GetEnv(key string) string {\n\tv, _ := envValue(key)\n\treturn v\n}\n\n// envValue returns the value of key in the manigot checkout's .env, reporting\n// whether the key was present at all (an explicit \"KEY=\" counts as\n// present-but-empty, which matters once the file is sourced: sourcing\n// overwrites an inherited value with the empty one).\nfunc envValue(key string) (string, bool) {\n\tdata, err := os.ReadFile(EnvFile())\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn \"\", false\n\t}\n\tfor _, line := range strings.Split(string(data), \"\\n\") {\n\t\tline = strings.TrimSpace(line)\n\t\tif line == \"\" || strings.HasPrefix(line, \"#\") {\n\t\t\tcontinue\n\t\t}\n\t\tline = strings.TrimPrefix(line, \"export \")\n\t\tk, value, ok := strings.Cut(line, \"=\")\n\t\tif !ok || strings.TrimSpace(k) != key {\n\t\t\tcontinue\n\t\t}\n\t\treturn unquoteEnvValue(strings.TrimSpace(value)), true\n\t}\n\treturn \"\", false\n}\n\n// EnvValue returns the effective value of key the way the old scripts saw it:\n// the .env value when the key is present there (sourcing exports it into the\n// process environment, overwriting anything inherited), else the process\n// environment. The ported CLI commands use it for readiness checks so an\n// inherited export counts as configured, exactly like the scripts' `${!KEY:-}`\n// reads did.\nfunc EnvValue(key string) string {\n\tif v, ok := envValue(key); ok {\n\t\treturn v\n\t}\n\treturn os.Getenv(key)\n}\n\n// unquoteEnvValue strips one pair of surrounding double or single quotes from\n// an .env value, mirroring how the shell would source it.\nfunc unquoteEnvValue(value string) string {\n\tif len(value) >= 2 {\n\t\tif (value[0] == '\"' && value[len(value)-1] == '\"') ||\n\t\t\t(value[0] == '\\'' && value[len(value)-1] == '\\'') {\n\t\t\treturn value[1 : len(value)-1]\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn value\n}\n\n// UpsertEnv upserts key=value into the manigot checkout's .env, preserving\n// every other line (credentials, comments, model overrides). The file is\n// created with the standard header when it does not exist yet. value is\n// written unquoted, matching the format config's own reader tolerates.\n// It is the generic form of writeEnvProfile; the ported CLI commands use it to\n// write any credential or model key.\nfunc UpsertEnv(key, value string) error {\n\tpath := EnvFile()\n\tif path == \"\" {\n\t\treturn fmt.Errorf(\"cannot determine the manigot checkout to write %s into (set $%s)\", key, home.EnvHome)\n\t}\n\n\tcontent, err := os.ReadFile(path)\n\tvar lines []string\n\tswitch {\n\tcase err == nil:\n\t\tlines = strings.Split(string(content), \"\\n\")\n\tcase os.IsNotExist(err):\n\t\t// Seed a fresh file with the same header scripts/profiles.sh and\n\t\t// scripts/setup.sh write.\n\t\tlines = []string{\"# manigot configuration — credentials and defaults (never commit this file)\"}\n\tdefault:\n\t\treturn err\n\t}\n\n\treplaced := false\n\tfor i, line := range lines {\n\t\ttrimmed := strings.TrimSpace(line)\n\t\tif trimmed == \"\" || strings.HasPrefix(trimmed, \"#\") {\n\t\t\tcontinue\n\t\t}\n\t\tif k, _, ok := strings.Cut(strings.TrimPrefix(trimmed, \"export \"), \"=\"); ok && strings.TrimSpace(k) == key {\n\t\t\tlines[i] = key + \"=\" + value\n\t\t\treplaced = true\n\t\t\tbreak\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\tif !replaced {\n\t\tif last := lines[len(lines)-1]; last == \"\" {\n\t\t\t// The file ended with a newline — reuse the trailing empty\n\t\t\t// element rather than leaving a blank line above the append.\n\t\t\tlines[len(lines)-1] = key + \"=\" + value\n\t\t} else {\n\t\t\t// No trailing newline — add one so the new line starts fresh.\n\t\t\tlines = append(lines, \"\", key+\"=\"+value)\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\n\treturn os.WriteFile(path, []byte(strings.Join(lines, \"\\n\")), 0o644)\n}\n\n// readEnvProfile returns the MANIGOT_PROFILE value from manigot/.env (\"\" when\n// absent or unreadable). It is a best-effort scan tolerant of shell-isms in\n// the file: a missing .env is the normal pre-first\n// save state and the caller falls back to the legacy tui-settings.json fields\n// and finally to ProfileClaudePro. Lines may carry an `export ` prefix or\n// surrounding quotes — both are tolerated.\nfunc readEnvProfile() string {\n\treturn GetEnv(\"MANIGOT_PROFILE\")\n}\n\n// writeEnvProfile upserts MANIGOT_PROFILE=<profile> into manigot/.env,\n// preserving every other line (credentials, comments, model overrides). The\n// file is created with the standard header when it does not exist yet.\n// profile must already be validated by the caller (see Save); it is written\n// unquoted, matching the format config's own reader tolerates.\nfunc writeEnvProfile(profile string) error {\n\treturn UpsertEnv(\"MANIGOT_PROFILE\", profile)\n}\n\n// Dir returns the directory the settings file lives in: config/ inside the\n// manigot checkout the running binary belongs to (see home.Root). It is\n// \"\" if that checkout could not be located.\nfunc Dir() string {\n\thome := home.Root()\n\tif home == \"\" {\n\t\treturn \"\"\n\t}\n\treturn filepath.Join(home, \"config\")\n}\n\n// Path returns the settings file's path, or \"\" if Dir does.\nfunc Path() string {\n\tdir := Dir()\n\tif dir == \"\" {\n\t\treturn \"\"\n\t}\n\treturn filepath.Join(dir, \"tui-settings.json\")\n}\n\n// Load reads the settings. A file that does not exist yet (nothing has been\n// saved, or the checkout could not be located) is not an error — it yields the\n// zero-value Settings, which every caller treats as \"defaults\".\n//\n// Profile resolution order: MANIGOT_PROFILE in manigot/.env — the shared,\n// central store, written by both `mg profiles` and the TUI's settings screen —\n// wins when present and valid. Failing that, the legacy `profile` field of\n// tui-settings.json (and the even older `tool` field, migrated:\n// \"claude-code\" maps to ProfileClaudePro, \"opencode\" maps to ProfileZAI) is\n// honored as a migration fallback so an existing user's choice survives the\n// upgrade. Neither legacy field is written back — Save persists the profile to\n// .env only.\nfunc Load() (Settings, error) {\n\tpath := Path()\n\tvar s Settings\n\tif path != \"\" {\n\t\tdata, err := os.ReadFile(path)\n\t\tswitch {\n\t\tcase err == nil:\n\t\t\tif err := json.Unmarshal(data, &s); err != nil {\n\t\t\t\treturn Settings{}, fmt.Errorf(\"%s: %w\", path, err)\n\t\t\t}\n\t\tcase os.IsNotExist(err):\n\t\t\t// nothing saved yet — zero value, defaults apply\n\t\tdefault:\n\t\t\treturn Settings{}, err\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\n\tif envProfile := readEnvProfile(); envProfile != \"\" {\n\t\tif _, ok := ProfileByID(envProfile); ok {\n\t\t\ts.Profile = envProfile\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\tif s.Profile == \"\" && s.Tool != \"\" {\n\t\tswitch s.Tool {\n\t\tcase ToolOpenCode:\n\t\t\ts.Profile = ProfileZAI\n\t\tdefault:\n\t\t\ts.Profile = ProfileClaudePro\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\ts.Tool = \"\" // legacy field — not written back\n\ts.Theme = GetEnv(\"OPENCODE_THEME\")\n\treturn s, nil\n}\n\n// Save writes s to disk: the editor to config/tui-settings.json (creating\n// config/ if needed) and the profile to manigot/.env as MANIGOT_PROFILE —\n// the shared default bare `mg` and the TUI both use. The profile is no longer\n// written to tui-settings.json: that field (like the older `tool` field) is\n// legacy, read for migration by Load only.\nfunc Save(s Settings) error {\n\tdir := Dir()\n\tif dir == \"\" {\n\t\treturn fmt.Errorf(\"cannot determine the manigot checkout to save settings into (set $%s)\", home.EnvHome)\n\t}\n\tif err := os.MkdirAll(dir, 0o755); err != nil {\n\t\treturn err\n\t}\n\tlegacy := s\n\tlegacy.Profile = \"\" // not persisted here anymore — `omitempty` drops it\n\tdata, err := json.MarshalIndent(legacy, \"\", \"  \")\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn err\n\t}\n\tif err := os.WriteFile(filepath.Join(dir, \"tui-settings.json\"), data, 0o644); err != nil {\n\t\treturn err\n\t}\n\n\tprofile := s.ProfileValue()\n\tif _, ok := ProfileByID(profile); !ok {\n\t\treturn fmt.Errorf(\"cannot save: %q is not a valid profile id\", profile)\n\t}\n\tif err := writeEnvProfile(profile); err != nil {\n\t\treturn err\n\t}\n\treturn UpsertEnv(\"OPENCODE_THEME\", s.Theme)\n}\n","numLines":623,"startLine":1,"totalLines":623}}}
