{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01YDbvmuX4esxWB2HZZg3k2k","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t// Host mode (mg host) is the counterpart of the docker session path in\n2\t// docker.go: instead of assembling a `docker run` argv, it assembles a direct\n3\t// invocation of the profile's agent CLI (claude / opencode) running on the\n4\t// host itself — no container, no mounts, no isolation. Everything before the\n5\t// launch (ParseArgs, ResolveProfile, ResolveRoot, CheckAuth) is shared; only\n6\t// the last step differs.\n7\t//\n8\t// Host mode is for work that must happen on the host — outside the container.\n9\t// The CLIs are launched exactly as installed on the host: no auto-approval\n10\t// flags (--dangerously-skip-permissions / --auto are only safe inside the\n11\t// isolated, ephemeral container), so the user supervises every tool call.\n12\tpackage session\n13\t\n14\timport (\n15\t\t\"fmt\"\n16\t\t\"io\"\n17\t\t\"os\"\n18\t\t\"os/exec\"\n19\t\t\"path/filepath\"\n20\t\t\"strings\"\n21\t\n22\t\t\"github.com/lmuskalla/manigot/internal/config\"\n23\t\t\"github.com/lmuskalla/manigot/internal/fs\"\n24\t\t\"github.com/lmuskalla/manigot/internal/home\"\n25\t)\n26\t\n27\t// HostInvocation is a fully assembled direct CLI invocation for host mode.\n28\ttype HostInvocation struct {\n29\t\t// Argv is the complete argument vector: [\"claude\", ...] or\n30\t\t// [\"opencode\", ...].\n31\t\tArgv []string\n32\t\n33\t\t// Dir is the working directory the CLI runs from: the resolved project\n34\t\t// root, or the job's worktree once --job has resolved one.\n35\t\tDir string\n36\t\n37\t\t// Env is the child process environment: the host environment plus the\n38\t\t// profile's credential keys (see hostEnv).\n39\t\tEnv []string\n40\t}\n41\t\n42\t// hostBinaryName maps the profile's tool id (config.ToolClaudeCode /\n43\t// config.ToolOpenCode) to the binary installed on the host. The tool ids are\n44\t// manigot's own names (\"claude-code\"); the binaries are \"claude\"/\"opencode\".\n45\tfunc hostBinaryName(tool string) string {\n46\t\tif tool == config.ToolOpenCode {\n47\t\t\treturn \"opencode\"\n48\t\t}\n49\t\treturn \"claude\"\n50\t}\n51\t\n52\t// hostLookPath is exec.LookPath, split out so tests can point it at stub\n53\t// binaries (or a failure) instead of requiring claude/opencode on the test\n54\t// machine's PATH. Mirrors internal/launch's ExeOverride pattern.\n55\tvar hostLookPath = exec.LookPath\n56\t\n57\t// BuildHostInvocation assembles the direct CLI argv for mg host, mirroring\n58\t// BuildDockerInvocation's structure minus everything docker-specific: no\n59\t// mounts, no .env shadowing, no git-identity forwarding, no quote, no\n60\t// auto-approval flags. Diagnostics (banner, warnings) go to diag — always\n61\t// stderr, per the same convention as the docker builder.\n62\tfunc BuildHostInvocation(opts Options, info ProfileInfo, root Root, diag io.Writer) (HostInvocation, error) {\n63\t\t// --print is the non-interactive container path (bare `mg --print` and\n64\t\t// mg jdi, whose orchestration parses the container output contract). Host\n65\t\t// mode is for manual, interactive work — reject it outright rather than\n66\t\t// silently starting an interactive CLI under an unattended caller.\n67\t\tif opts.Print {\n68\t\t\treturn HostInvocation{}, fmt.Errorf(\"--print is not supported with mg host — host sessions run the CLI directly for interactive work.\\nUse the docker session (bare `mg --print`) or 'mg jdi' for non-interactive runs.\")\n69\t\t}\n70\t\n71\t\t// The .env warning run.sh printed when the file was missing — host mode\n72\t\t// reads the same credentials (config.EnvValue prefers the checkout's .env).\n73\t\tif envFile := config.EnvFile(); envFile != \"\" {\n74\t\t\tif _, err := os.Stat(envFile); err != nil {\n75\t\t\t\tfmt.Fprintf(diag, \"Warning: no .env found at %s\\n\", envFile)\n76\t\t\t}\n77\t\t}\n78\t\n79\t\t// mg host is just a launcher — the CLI must exist on the host. The docker\n80\t\t// path needs no such check: both CLIs are baked into the image.\n81\t\tbinary := hostBinaryName(info.Tool)\n82\t\tif _, err := hostLookPath(binary); err != nil {\n83\t\t\treturn HostInvocation{}, fmt.Errorf(\"%s is not installed on the host — mg host runs the CLI directly, without a container.\\nInstall it, or use the docker session (bare mg) instead.\", binary)\n84\t\t}\n85\t\n86\t\t// ── Global agents ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n87\t\t// mg host runs the CLI directly with no image/mounts, so the host CLI\n88\t\t// cannot see the manigot checkout's global agents (<home>/agents/) the way\n89\t\t// the container path mounts them. Surface them into the CLI's own global\n90\t\t// agents dir (~/.claude/agents/ for Claude Code,\n91\t\t// ~/.config/opencode/agents/ for OpenCode) — symlinked raw for Claude Code\n92\t\t// (the list form is its native schema), converted copies for OpenCode\n93\t\t// (which hard-errors on the list-form tools: key — see convertAgentFile).\n94\t\t// Non-destructive: a name already present in the target dir is left\n95\t\t// untouched (the user's own host agent wins), and nothing happens when the\n96\t\t// checkout has no agents/ dir — so a best-effort failure only warns and\n97\t\t// never blocks the session.\n98\t\tif n, err := installHostGlobalAgents(info.Tool); err != nil {\n99\t\t\tfmt.Fprintf(diag, \"Warning: could not make global agents available to %s on the host: %v\\n\", binary, err)\n100\t\t} else if n > 0 {\n101\t\t\tfmt.Fprintf(diag, \"  Installed : %d global agent(s) into %s's host config\\n\", n, binary)\n102\t\t}\n103\t\n104\t\t// ── Global skills ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n105\t\t// The same delivery problem applies to skills: the host CLI cannot see the\n106\t\t// checkout's global skills (<home>/skills/) without the image/mounts, so\n107\t\t// they are surfaced into the CLI's own global skills dir — symlinked dirs\n108\t\t// for Claude Code, copied dirs for OpenCode (see installHostGlobalSkills).\n109\t\t// Non-destructive and warn-only, exactly like the agents install above.\n110\t\tif n, err := installHostGlobalSkills(info.Tool); err != nil {\n111\t\t\tfmt.Fprintf(diag, \"Warning: could not make global skills available to %s on the host: %v\\n\", binary, err)\n112\t\t} else if n > 0 {\n113\t\t\tfmt.Fprintf(diag, \"  Installed : %d global skill(s) into %s's host config\\n\", n, binary)\n114\t\t}\n115\t\n116\t\t// ── Global meta prompt ──────────────────────────────────────────────────\n117\t\t// The same delivery problem applies to the system-wide meta prompt\n118\t\t// (<home>/prompts/meta.md): the host CLI cannot see it without the image/mounts,\n119\t\t// so it is surfaced into the CLI's own global instruction file. Unlike\n120\t\t// agents/skills it is a single file copied, never symlinked, so Claude's\n121\t\t// /memory writes and agent edits can never reach the checkout. Non-\n122\t\t// clobbering and warn-only, exactly like the agents/skills installs above.\n123\t\tif n, err := installHostGlobalMeta(info.Tool); err != nil {\n124\t\t\tfmt.Fprintf(diag, \"Warning: could not install the global meta prompt for %s on the host: %v\\n\", binary, err)\n125\t\t} else if n > 0 {\n126\t\t\tfmt.Fprintf(diag, \"  Installed : global meta prompt into %s's host config\\n\", binary)\n127\t\t}\n128\t\n129\t\t// ── Job prompt ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n130\t\t// Host-pathed variant of docker.go's container job prompt: the CLI runs on\n131\t\t// the host, so the job path it is told to work on must be a host path.\n132\t\tinitialPrompt := \"\"\n133\t\tif root.Job != \"\" {\n134\t\t\thostJobDir := filepath.Join(root.ProjectRoot, \"docs\", \"jobs\", root.Job)\n135\t\t\tinitialPrompt = \"Please work on the job at \" + hostJobDir + \" — start by reading brief.md\"\n136\t\t} else if opts.Prompt != \"\" {\n137\t\t\tinitialPrompt = opts.Prompt\n138\t\t}\n139\t\n140\t\tvar agentFlag []string\n141\t\tif opts.Agent != \"\" {\n142\t\t\tagentFlag = []string{\"--agent\", opts.Agent}\n143\t\t}\n144\t\n145\t\t// Claude takes the prompt positionally; OpenCode as --prompt — the same\n146\t\t// per-tool routing the docker path uses.\n147\t\tvar promptArgs []string\n148\t\tif initialPrompt != \"\" {\n149\t\t\tif info.Tool == config.ToolOpenCode {\n150\t\t\t\tpromptArgs = []string{\"--prompt\", initialPrompt}\n151\t\t\t} else {\n152\t\t\t\tpromptArgs = []string{initialPrompt}\n153\t\t\t}\n154\t\t}\n155\t\n156\t\t// ── Model ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n157\t\t// The docker path forwards OPENCODE_MODEL and scripts/entrypoint.sh writes\n158\t\t// it into an opencode config inside the container. On the host mg must not\n159\t\t// write the user's real ~/.config/opencode/opencode.json, so the effective\n160\t\t// model is forwarded via opencode's own --model flag instead (verified\n161\t\t// supported: `opencode --help` shows -m/--model). OPENCODE_MODEL itself is\n162\t\t// deliberately not put into the child env — opencode does not read it.\n163\t\t// Claude Code takes the same --model flag directly (see docker.go); mg\n164\t\t// host forwards ClaudeModel the same way, no config file involved.\n165\t\tvar modelArgs []string\n166\t\tif info.Tool == config.ToolOpenCode && info.OpenCodeModel != \"\" {\n167\t\t\tmodelArgs = []string{\"--model\", info.OpenCodeModel}\n168\t\t} else if info.Tool == config.ToolClaudeCode && info.ClaudeModel != \"\" {\n169\t\t\tmodelArgs = []string{\"--model\", info.ClaudeModel}\n170\t\t}\n171\t\n172\t\t// ── Info banner ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n173\t\tfmt.Fprintln(diag, \"╔══════════════════════════════════════╗\")\n174\t\tfmt.Fprintln(diag, \"║           manigot                   ║\")\n175\t\tfmt.Fprintln(diag, \"╠══════════════════════════════════════╣\")\n176\t\tfmt.Fprintln(diag, \"║  Entering host mode (no docker container)...\")\n177\t\tfmt.Fprintf(diag, \"║  Project : %s\\n\", filepath.Base(root.InvocationRoot))\n178\t\tfmt.Fprintf(diag, \"║  Root    : %s\\n\", root.ProjectRoot)\n179\t\tif root.DocsInitialized {\n180\t\t\tfmt.Fprintf(diag, \"║  Docs    : %s\\n\", root.DocsDir)\n181\t\t} else {\n182\t\t\tfmt.Fprintln(diag, \"║  Docs    : (none — job workflow unavailable, running off the books)\")\n183\t\t}\n184\t\tif info.Profile != \"\" {\n185\t\t\tfmt.Fprintf(diag, \"║  Profile : %s\\n\", info.Profile)\n186\t\t}\n187\t\tif info.Tool != \"\" {\n188\t\t\tfmt.Fprintf(diag, \"║  Tool    : %s\\n\", info.Tool)\n189\t\t}\n190\t\tif opts.Agent != \"\" {\n191\t\t\tfmt.Fprintf(diag, \"║  Agent   : %s\\n\", opts.Agent)\n192\t\t}\n193\t\tif root.Job != \"\" {\n194\t\t\tfmt.Fprintf(diag, \"║  Job     : %s\\n\", root.Job)\n195\t\t}\n196\t\tfmt.Fprintln(diag, \"╚══════════════════════════════════════╝\")\n197\t\tfmt.Fprintln(diag, \"\")\n198\t\n199\t\t// ── Assemble the argv ───────────────────────────────────────────────────\n200\t\t// Deliberately no --dangerously-skip-permissions (Claude) / --auto\n201\t\t// (OpenCode): safe only inside the isolated, ephemeral container. On the\n202\t\t// host the CLI keeps its normal per-tool confirmation prompts.\n203\t\targv := []string{binary}\n204\t\targv = append(argv, agentFlag...)\n205\t\targv = append(argv, modelArgs...)\n206\t\targv = append(argv, promptArgs...)\n207\t\targv = append(argv, opts.Pass...)\n208\t\n209\t\treturn HostInvocation{\n210\t\t\tArgv: argv,\n211\t\t\tDir:  root.ProjectRoot,\n212\t\t\tEnv:  hostEnv(info),\n213\t\t}, nil\n214\t}\n215\t\n216\t// hostEnv builds the child process environment for a host invocation: the\n217\t// host's own environment plus the profile's credential keys (the KEY=VALUE\n218\t// strings behind ProfileInfo.KeyEnv's docker -e pairs). Appended last, so\n219\t// exec's duplicate-key handling (the last value wins) resolves every key to\n220\t// the .env-effective value the session flow already validated — the same\n221\t// credentials the container path forwards. OPENCODE_MODEL is excluded: it is\n222\t// forwarded as --model instead (see BuildHostInvocation), and opencode does\n223\t// not read it from the environment. OPENCODE_THEME is excluded too: it is a\n224\t// container-only knob — the docker path applies it by writing a generated\n225\t// ~/.config/opencode/tui.json inside the ephemeral container (see\n226\t// scripts/entrypoint.sh) — and mg host runs the CLI against the user's own\n227\t// real ~/.config/opencode, which already reflects whatever theme the user\n228\t// configured there (the same reason mg host needs no other theme handling;\n229\t// see tasks.md's scope note for this job). Forwarding it would be a silent,\n230\t// untested, unused env leak.\n231\t//\n232\t// For OpenCode sessions, TMUX/TMUX_PANE are filtered out of the child env —\n233\t// the same exception the docker path applies (see the terminalEnvVars comment\n234\t// in docker.go): when OpenCode sees TMUX set it wraps its OSC 52 clipboard\n235\t// write in tmux's DCS-passthrough escape, which default tmux configuration\n236\t// discards entirely, so the host clipboard is never touched. Stripping TMUX\n237\t// makes OpenCode emit plain OSC 52, which tmux's set-clipboard on handles.\n238\t// Claude Code's host env stays the full host environment.\n239\tfunc hostEnv(info ProfileInfo) []string {\n240\t\tenv := os.Environ()\n241\t\tfor i := 0; i+1 < len(info.KeyEnv); i += 2 {\n242\t\t\tif info.KeyEnv[i] != \"-e\" {\n243\t\t\t\tcontinue\n244\t\t\t}\n245\t\t\tpair := info.KeyEnv[i+1]\n246\t\t\tif strings.HasPrefix(pair, \"OPENCODE_MODEL=\") || strings.HasPrefix(pair, \"OPENCODE_THEME=\") {\n247\t\t\t\tcontinue\n248\t\t\t}\n249\t\t\tenv = append(env, pair)\n250\t\t}\n251\t\tif info.Tool == config.ToolOpenCode {\n252\t\t\tvar filtered []string\n253\t\t\tfor _, kv := range env {\n254\t\t\t\tif strings.HasPrefix(kv, \"TMUX=\") || strings.HasPrefix(kv, \"TMUX_PANE=\") {\n255\t\t\t\t\tcontinue\n256\t\t\t\t}\n257\t\t\t\tfiltered = append(filtered, kv)\n258\t\t\t}\n259\t\t\tenv = filtered\n260\t\t}\n261\t\treturn env\n262\t}\n263\t\n264\t// hostGlobalAgentsDir resolves the host CLI's global agents directory for a\n265\t// tool: ~/.claude/agents/ for Claude Code, ~/.config/opencode/agents/ for\n266\t// OpenCode (the same locations the container path mounts the global agents\n267\t// into). The dir is derived from $HOME (os.UserHomeDir), so tests can point it\n268\t// at a temp dir.\n269\tfunc hostGlobalAgentsDir(tool string) string {\n270\t\thomeDir, err := os.UserHomeDir()\n271\t\tif err != nil {\n272\t\t\thomeDir = os.Getenv(\"HOME\")\n273\t\t}\n274\t\tif tool == config.ToolOpenCode {\n275\t\t\treturn filepath.Join(homeDir, \".config\", \"opencode\", \"agents\")\n276\t\t}\n277\t\treturn filepath.Join(homeDir, \".claude\", \"agents\")\n278\t}\n279\t\n280\t// installHostGlobalAgents surfaces the manigot checkout's global agents\n281\t// (<home>/agents/) to the host CLI — the host equivalent of the container\n282\t// path's read-only mount (mg host runs the CLI directly, with no mounts).\n283\t//\n284\t// Delivery is per-tool, mirroring the container path's conversion split:\n285\t//\n286\t//   - Claude Code: each agent file is symlinked into ~/.claude/agents/ raw —\n287\t//     the list-form frontmatter (name:/description:/tools: Read, Grep, ...)\n288\t//     is Claude's native subagent schema. The symlinks point at the live\n289\t//     checkout files, so edits to agents/ are reflected without re-installing.\n290\t//   - OpenCode: OpenCode hard-errors on the list-form tools: key, so the raw\n291\t//     file cannot be symlinked — each agent is converted first (name:/tools:\n292\t//     stripped, permission: passed through — see convertAgentFile) and the\n293\t//     converted copy is written into ~/.config/opencode/agents/.\n294\t//\n295\t// It never clobbers existing host agent config: a name already present in the\n296\t// target dir is left untouched (the user's own host agent wins, mirroring the\n297\t// project-overrides-global precedence). One exception: a symlink pointing at\n298\t// the checkout's own agents/<name> is this installer's stale raw OpenCode link\n299\t// from before the conversion fix, and is replaced with a converted copy so it\n300\t// stops hard-erroring OpenCode. Nothing is created when the checkout has no\n301\t// agents/ dir or no home can be located. Returns the number of agents\n302\t// installed.\n303\tfunc installHostGlobalAgents(tool string) (int, error) {\n304\t\thomeDir := home.Root()\n305\t\tif homeDir == \"\" {\n306\t\t\treturn 0, nil\n307\t\t}\n308\t\tsrcDir := filepath.Join(homeDir, \"agents\")\n309\t\tentries, err := os.ReadDir(srcDir)\n310\t\tif err != nil {\n311\t\t\tif os.IsNotExist(err) {\n312\t\t\t\treturn 0, nil\n313\t\t\t}\n314\t\t\treturn 0, err\n315\t\t}\n316\t\tvar names []string\n317\t\tfor _, e := range entries {\n318\t\t\tif e.IsDir() || !strings.HasSuffix(e.Name(), \".md\") {\n319\t\t\t\tcontinue\n320\t\t\t}\n321\t\t\tnames = append(names, e.Name())\n322\t\t}\n323\t\tif len(names) == 0 {\n324\t\t\treturn 0, nil\n325\t\t}\n326\t\n327\t\ttargetDir := hostGlobalAgentsDir(tool)\n328\t\tif err := os.MkdirAll(targetDir, 0o755); err != nil {\n329\t\t\treturn 0, err\n330\t\t}\n331\t\tinstalled := 0\n332\t\tfor _, name := range names {\n333\t\t\ttarget := filepath.Join(targetDir, name)\n334\t\t\tif tool == config.ToolOpenCode {\n335\t\t\t\t// OpenCode hard-errors on the list-form tools: key (see\n336\t\t\t\t// convertAgents), so the raw checkout file cannot be symlinked:\n337\t\t\t\t// the host target gets a converted copy instead. Never clobber an\n338\t\t\t\t// existing host agent — except this installer's own stale raw\n339\t\t\t\t// symlink (one pointing at the checkout's agents/<name>), which is\n340\t\t\t\t// replaced with the converted copy.\n341\t\t\t\tif fi, err := os.Lstat(target); err == nil {\n342\t\t\t\t\tif fi.Mode()&os.ModeSymlink != 0 {\n343\t\t\t\t\t\tif dest, rerr := os.Readlink(target); rerr == nil && dest == filepath.Join(srcDir, name) {\n344\t\t\t\t\t\t\tos.Remove(target) // our stale raw link — replaced below\n345\t\t\t\t\t\t} else {\n346\t\t\t\t\t\t\tcontinue // a foreign symlink — the user's setup wins\n347\t\t\t\t\t\t}\n348\t\t\t\t\t} else {\n349\t\t\t\t\t\tcontinue // a regular file — the user's own agent wins\n350\t\t\t\t\t}\n351\t\t\t\t}\n352\t\t\t\tdata, rerr := os.ReadFile(filepath.Join(srcDir, name))\n353\t\t\t\tif rerr != nil {\n354\t\t\t\t\tcontinue\n355\t\t\t\t}\n356\t\t\t\tif werr := os.WriteFile(target, convertAgentFile(data), 0o644); werr != nil {\n357\t\t\t\t\tcontinue\n358\t\t\t\t}\n359\t\t\t\tinstalled++\n360\t\t\t\tcontinue\n361\t\t\t}\n362\t\t\t// Claude Code — raw symlink (list form is Claude's native schema).\n363\t\t\t// Never clobber an existing host agent config — the user's own file\n364\t\t\t// (or an existing symlink, dangling or not) wins.\n365\t\t\tif _, err := os.Lstat(target); err == nil {\n366\t\t\t\tcontinue\n367\t\t\t}\n368\t\t\tif err := os.Symlink(filepath.Join(srcDir, name), target); err != nil {\n369\t\t\t\t// Skip names we couldn't link but keep going for the rest.\n370\t\t\t\tcontinue\n371\t\t\t}\n372\t\t\tinstalled++\n373\t\t}\n374\t\treturn installed, nil\n375\t}\n376\t\n377\t// hostGlobalSkillsDir resolves the host CLI's global skills directory for a\n378\t// tool: ~/.claude/skills/ for Claude Code, ~/.config/opencode/skills/ for\n379\t// OpenCode (the same locations the container path mounts the global skills\n380\t// into). The dir is derived from $HOME (os.UserHomeDir), so tests can point it\n381\t// at a temp dir.\n382\tfunc hostGlobalSkillsDir(tool string) string {\n383\t\thomeDir, err := os.UserHomeDir()\n384\t\tif err != nil {\n385\t\t\thomeDir = os.Getenv(\"HOME\")\n386\t\t}\n387\t\tif tool == config.ToolOpenCode {\n388\t\t\treturn filepath.Join(homeDir, \".config\", \"opencode\", \"skills\")\n389\t\t}\n390\t\treturn filepath.Join(homeDir, \".claude\", \"skills\")\n391\t}\n392\t\n393\t// installHostGlobalSkills surfaces the manigot checkout's global skills\n394\t// (<home>/skills/) to the host CLI — the host equivalent of the container\n395\t// path's read-only mount (mg host runs the CLI directly, with no mounts).\n396\t//\n397\t// A skill is a directory (SKILL.md plus optional support files), so delivery\n398\t// is per-directory, mirroring installHostGlobalAgents' per-tool split:\n399\t//\n400\t//   - Claude Code: each skill dir is symlinked into ~/.claude/skills/ — the\n401\t//     symlinks point at the live checkout dirs, so edits to skills/ are\n402\t//     reflected without re-installing.\n403\t//   - OpenCode: each skill dir is copied into ~/.config/opencode/skills/ — a\n404\t//     self-contained snapshot rather than a symlink, so the CLI's skills dir\n405\t//     never points into (or writes into) the manigot checkout.\n406\t//\n407\t// It never clobbers existing host skill config: a name already present in the\n408\t// target dir is left untouched (the user's own host skill wins, mirroring the\n409\t// project-overrides-global precedence). Nothing is created when the checkout\n410\t// has no skills/ dir or no home can be located. Returns the number of skills\n411\t// installed.\n412\tfunc installHostGlobalSkills(tool string) (int, error) {\n413\t\thomeDir := home.Root()\n414\t\tif homeDir == \"\" {\n415\t\t\treturn 0, nil\n416\t\t}\n417\t\tsrcDir := filepath.Join(homeDir, \"skills\")\n418\t\tskills, err := listSkills(srcDir)\n419\t\tif err != nil {\n420\t\t\treturn 0, err\n421\t\t}\n422\t\tif len(skills) == 0 {\n423\t\t\treturn 0, nil\n424\t\t}\n425\t\n426\t\ttargetDir := hostGlobalSkillsDir(tool)\n427\t\tif err := os.MkdirAll(targetDir, 0o755); err != nil {\n428\t\t\treturn 0, err\n429\t\t}\n430\t\tinstalled := 0\n431\t\tfor _, s := range skills {\n432\t\t\ttarget := filepath.Join(targetDir, s.Name)\n433\t\t\t// Never clobber an existing host skill — the user's own dir (or an\n434\t\t\t// existing symlink, dangling or not) wins.\n435\t\t\tif _, err := os.Lstat(target); err == nil {\n436\t\t\t\tcontinue\n437\t\t\t}\n438\t\t\tif tool == config.ToolOpenCode {\n439\t\t\t\t// OpenCode gets a copied snapshot (see the doc comment above).\n440\t\t\t\tif err := copyDir(target, s.Dir); err != nil {\n441\t\t\t\t\tcontinue // skip names we couldn't copy, keep going for the rest\n442\t\t\t\t}\n443\t\t\t} else {\n444\t\t\t\t// Claude Code — raw symlink to the live checkout dir.\n445\t\t\t\tif err := os.Symlink(s.Dir, target); err != nil {\n446\t\t\t\t\tcontinue\n447\t\t\t\t}\n448\t\t\t}\n449\t\t\tinstalled++\n450\t\t}\n451\t\treturn installed, nil\n452\t}\n453\t\n454\t// hostGlobalMetaFile resolves the host CLI's global instruction file for a\n455\t// tool — the single file the CLI loads in every session at its *global\n456\t// instruction* location: ~/.claude/CLAUDE.md for Claude Code (the user-global\n457\t// memory file) and ~/.config/opencode/AGENTS.md for OpenCode (the global\n458\t// rules file) — the same locations the container path mounts <home>/prompts/meta.md\n459\t// into. The path is derived from $HOME (os.UserHomeDir), so tests can point it\n460\t// at a temp dir.\n461\tfunc hostGlobalMetaFile(tool string) string {\n462\t\thomeDir, err := os.UserHomeDir()\n463\t\tif err != nil {\n464\t\t\thomeDir = os.Getenv(\"HOME\")\n465\t\t}\n466\t\tif tool == config.ToolOpenCode {\n467\t\t\treturn filepath.Join(homeDir, \".config\", \"opencode\", \"AGENTS.md\")\n468\t\t}\n469\t\treturn filepath.Join(homeDir, \".claude\", \"CLAUDE.md\")\n470\t}\n471\t\n472\t// installHostGlobalMeta surfaces the manigot checkout's system-wide meta\n473\t// prompt (<home>/prompts/meta.md) to the host CLI — the host equivalent of the\n474\t// container path's read-only mount (mg host runs the CLI directly, with no\n475\t// mounts).\n476\t//\n477\t// It delivers a single file by COPY, never a symlink: ~/.claude/CLAUDE.md is\n478\t// Claude Code's user-writable memory file, so a symlink would let Claude's\n479\t// /memory writes and agent edits land back in the manigot checkout. A copied\n480\t// file keeps the host CLI's own global instruction file fully self-contained.\n481\t//\n482\t// It never clobbers an existing host file: a target that already exists (the\n483\t// user's own CLAUDE.md / AGENTS.md) wins and nothing is written. Nothing is\n484\t// created when the checkout has no prompts/meta.md or no home can be located. Returns\n485\t// 1 when the file was installed, 0 otherwise.\n486\tfunc installHostGlobalMeta(tool string) (int, error) {\n487\t\thomeDir := home.Root()\n488\t\tif homeDir == \"\" {\n489\t\t\treturn 0, nil\n490\t\t}\n491\t\tsrc := filepath.Join(homeDir, \"prompts\", \"meta.md\")\n492\t\tif !fs.IsFile(src) {\n493\t\t\treturn 0, nil\n494\t\t}\n495\t\n496\t\ttarget := hostGlobalMetaFile(tool)\n497\t\t// Never clobber an existing host instruction file — the user's own file\n498\t\t// wins.\n499\t\tif _, err := os.Lstat(target); err == nil {\n500\t\t\treturn 0, nil\n501\t\t}\n502\t\tif err := os.MkdirAll(filepath.Dir(target), 0o755); err != nil {\n503\t\t\treturn 0, err\n504\t\t}\n505\t\tdata, err := os.ReadFile(src)\n506\t\tif err != nil {\n507\t\t\treturn 0, err\n508\t\t}\n509\t\tif err := os.WriteFile(target, data, 0o644); err != nil {\n510\t\t\treturn 0, err\n511\t\t}\n512\t\treturn 1, nil\n513\t}\n514\t\n515\t// Run executes the assembled CLI invocation, wiring stdin/stdout/stderr\n516\t// through so Ctrl+C reaches the CLI directly (there is no container in\n517\t// between), and returns the CLI's exit code.\n518\tfunc (h HostInvocation) Run(stdin *os.File, stdout, stderr io.Writer) int {\n519\t\tcmd := exec.Command(h.Argv[0], h.Argv[1:]...)\n520\t\tcmd.Dir = h.Dir\n521\t\tcmd.Env = h.Env\n522\t\tcmd.Stdin = stdin\n523\t\tcmd.Stdout = stdout\n524\t\tcmd.Stderr = stderr\n525\t\tif err := cmd.Run(); err != nil {\n526\t\t\tif exitErr, ok := err.(*exec.ExitError); ok {\n527\t\t\t\treturn exitErr.ExitCode()\n528\t\t\t}\n529\t\t\tfmt.Fprintf(stderr, \"mg: %v\\n\", err)\n530\t\t\treturn 1\n531\t\t}\n532\t\treturn 0\n533\t}\n534\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4e2afcf5-7fc0-4a90-a6a6-e82e43a08449","uuid":"05b53d18-369d-47ac-9542-629b06784668","timestamp":"2026-08-31T13:46:10.949Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/workspace/src/internal/session/host.go","content":"// Host mode (mg host) is the counterpart of the docker session path in\n// docker.go: instead of assembling a `docker run` argv, it assembles a direct\n// invocation of the profile's agent CLI (claude / opencode) running on the\n// host itself — no container, no mounts, no isolation. Everything before the\n// launch (ParseArgs, ResolveProfile, ResolveRoot, CheckAuth) is shared; only\n// the last step differs.\n//\n// Host mode is for work that must happen on the host — outside the container.\n// The CLIs are launched exactly as installed on the host: no auto-approval\n// flags (--dangerously-skip-permissions / --auto are only safe inside the\n// isolated, ephemeral container), so the user supervises every tool call.\npackage session\n\nimport (\n\t\"fmt\"\n\t\"io\"\n\t\"os\"\n\t\"os/exec\"\n\t\"path/filepath\"\n\t\"strings\"\n\n\t\"github.com/lmuskalla/manigot/internal/config\"\n\t\"github.com/lmuskalla/manigot/internal/fs\"\n\t\"github.com/lmuskalla/manigot/internal/home\"\n)\n\n// HostInvocation is a fully assembled direct CLI invocation for host mode.\ntype HostInvocation struct {\n\t// Argv is the complete argument vector: [\"claude\", ...] or\n\t// [\"opencode\", ...].\n\tArgv []string\n\n\t// Dir is the working directory the CLI runs from: the resolved project\n\t// root, or the job's worktree once --job has resolved one.\n\tDir string\n\n\t// Env is the child process environment: the host environment plus the\n\t// profile's credential keys (see hostEnv).\n\tEnv []string\n}\n\n// hostBinaryName maps the profile's tool id (config.ToolClaudeCode /\n// config.ToolOpenCode) to the binary installed on the host. The tool ids are\n// manigot's own names (\"claude-code\"); the binaries are \"claude\"/\"opencode\".\nfunc hostBinaryName(tool string) string {\n\tif tool == config.ToolOpenCode {\n\t\treturn \"opencode\"\n\t}\n\treturn \"claude\"\n}\n\n// hostLookPath is exec.LookPath, split out so tests can point it at stub\n// binaries (or a failure) instead of requiring claude/opencode on the test\n// machine's PATH. Mirrors internal/launch's ExeOverride pattern.\nvar hostLookPath = exec.LookPath\n\n// BuildHostInvocation assembles the direct CLI argv for mg host, mirroring\n// BuildDockerInvocation's structure minus everything docker-specific: no\n// mounts, no .env shadowing, no git-identity forwarding, no quote, no\n// auto-approval flags. Diagnostics (banner, warnings) go to diag — always\n// stderr, per the same convention as the docker builder.\nfunc BuildHostInvocation(opts Options, info ProfileInfo, root Root, diag io.Writer) (HostInvocation, error) {\n\t// --print is the non-interactive container path (bare `mg --print` and\n\t// mg jdi, whose orchestration parses the container output contract). Host\n\t// mode is for manual, interactive work — reject it outright rather than\n\t// silently starting an interactive CLI under an unattended caller.\n\tif opts.Print {\n\t\treturn HostInvocation{}, fmt.Errorf(\"--print is not supported with mg host — host sessions run the CLI directly for interactive work.\\nUse the docker session (bare `mg --print`) or 'mg jdi' for non-interactive runs.\")\n\t}\n\n\t// The .env warning run.sh printed when the file was missing — host mode\n\t// reads the same credentials (config.EnvValue prefers the checkout's .env).\n\tif envFile := config.EnvFile(); envFile != \"\" {\n\t\tif _, err := os.Stat(envFile); err != nil {\n\t\t\tfmt.Fprintf(diag, \"Warning: no .env found at %s\\n\", envFile)\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\n\t// mg host is just a launcher — the CLI must exist on the host. The docker\n\t// path needs no such check: both CLIs are baked into the image.\n\tbinary := hostBinaryName(info.Tool)\n\tif _, err := hostLookPath(binary); err != nil {\n\t\treturn HostInvocation{}, fmt.Errorf(\"%s is not installed on the host — mg host runs the CLI directly, without a container.\\nInstall it, or use the docker session (bare mg) instead.\", binary)\n\t}\n\n\t// ── Global agents ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\t// mg host runs the CLI directly with no image/mounts, so the host CLI\n\t// cannot see the manigot checkout's global agents (<home>/agents/) the way\n\t// the container path mounts them. Surface them into the CLI's own global\n\t// agents dir (~/.claude/agents/ for Claude Code,\n\t// ~/.config/opencode/agents/ for OpenCode) — symlinked raw for Claude Code\n\t// (the list form is its native schema), converted copies for OpenCode\n\t// (which hard-errors on the list-form tools: key — see convertAgentFile).\n\t// Non-destructive: a name already present in the target dir is left\n\t// untouched (the user's own host agent wins), and nothing happens when the\n\t// checkout has no agents/ dir — so a best-effort failure only warns and\n\t// never blocks the session.\n\tif n, err := installHostGlobalAgents(info.Tool); err != nil {\n\t\tfmt.Fprintf(diag, \"Warning: could not make global agents available to %s on the host: %v\\n\", binary, err)\n\t} else if n > 0 {\n\t\tfmt.Fprintf(diag, \"  Installed : %d global agent(s) into %s's host config\\n\", n, binary)\n\t}\n\n\t// ── Global skills ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\t// The same delivery problem applies to skills: the host CLI cannot see the\n\t// checkout's global skills (<home>/skills/) without the image/mounts, so\n\t// they are surfaced into the CLI's own global skills dir — symlinked dirs\n\t// for Claude Code, copied dirs for OpenCode (see installHostGlobalSkills).\n\t// Non-destructive and warn-only, exactly like the agents install above.\n\tif n, err := installHostGlobalSkills(info.Tool); err != nil {\n\t\tfmt.Fprintf(diag, \"Warning: could not make global skills available to %s on the host: %v\\n\", binary, err)\n\t} else if n > 0 {\n\t\tfmt.Fprintf(diag, \"  Installed : %d global skill(s) into %s's host config\\n\", n, binary)\n\t}\n\n\t// ── Global meta prompt ──────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\t// The same delivery problem applies to the system-wide meta prompt\n\t// (<home>/prompts/meta.md): the host CLI cannot see it without the image/mounts,\n\t// so it is surfaced into the CLI's own global instruction file. Unlike\n\t// agents/skills it is a single file copied, never symlinked, so Claude's\n\t// /memory writes and agent edits can never reach the checkout. Non-\n\t// clobbering and warn-only, exactly like the agents/skills installs above.\n\tif n, err := installHostGlobalMeta(info.Tool); err != nil {\n\t\tfmt.Fprintf(diag, \"Warning: could not install the global meta prompt for %s on the host: %v\\n\", binary, err)\n\t} else if n > 0 {\n\t\tfmt.Fprintf(diag, \"  Installed : global meta prompt into %s's host config\\n\", binary)\n\t}\n\n\t// ── Job prompt ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\t// Host-pathed variant of docker.go's container job prompt: the CLI runs on\n\t// the host, so the job path it is told to work on must be a host path.\n\tinitialPrompt := \"\"\n\tif root.Job != \"\" {\n\t\thostJobDir := filepath.Join(root.ProjectRoot, \"docs\", \"jobs\", root.Job)\n\t\tinitialPrompt = \"Please work on the job at \" + hostJobDir + \" — start by reading brief.md\"\n\t} else if opts.Prompt != \"\" {\n\t\tinitialPrompt = opts.Prompt\n\t}\n\n\tvar agentFlag []string\n\tif opts.Agent != \"\" {\n\t\tagentFlag = []string{\"--agent\", opts.Agent}\n\t}\n\n\t// Claude takes the prompt positionally; OpenCode as --prompt — the same\n\t// per-tool routing the docker path uses.\n\tvar promptArgs []string\n\tif initialPrompt != \"\" {\n\t\tif info.Tool == config.ToolOpenCode {\n\t\t\tpromptArgs = []string{\"--prompt\", initialPrompt}\n\t\t} else {\n\t\t\tpromptArgs = []string{initialPrompt}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\n\t// ── Model ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\t// The docker path forwards OPENCODE_MODEL and scripts/entrypoint.sh writes\n\t// it into an opencode config inside the container. On the host mg must not\n\t// write the user's real ~/.config/opencode/opencode.json, so the effective\n\t// model is forwarded via opencode's own --model flag instead (verified\n\t// supported: `opencode --help` shows -m/--model). OPENCODE_MODEL itself is\n\t// deliberately not put into the child env — opencode does not read it.\n\t// Claude Code takes the same --model flag directly (see docker.go); mg\n\t// host forwards ClaudeModel the same way, no config file involved.\n\tvar modelArgs []string\n\tif info.Tool == config.ToolOpenCode && info.OpenCodeModel != \"\" {\n\t\tmodelArgs = []string{\"--model\", info.OpenCodeModel}\n\t} else if info.Tool == config.ToolClaudeCode && info.ClaudeModel != \"\" {\n\t\tmodelArgs = []string{\"--model\", info.ClaudeModel}\n\t}\n\n\t// ── Info banner ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\tfmt.Fprintln(diag, \"╔══════════════════════════════════════╗\")\n\tfmt.Fprintln(diag, \"║           manigot                   ║\")\n\tfmt.Fprintln(diag, \"╠══════════════════════════════════════╣\")\n\tfmt.Fprintln(diag, \"║  Entering host mode (no docker container)...\")\n\tfmt.Fprintf(diag, \"║  Project : %s\\n\", filepath.Base(root.InvocationRoot))\n\tfmt.Fprintf(diag, \"║  Root    : %s\\n\", root.ProjectRoot)\n\tif root.DocsInitialized {\n\t\tfmt.Fprintf(diag, \"║  Docs    : %s\\n\", root.DocsDir)\n\t} else {\n\t\tfmt.Fprintln(diag, \"║  Docs    : (none — job workflow unavailable, running off the books)\")\n\t}\n\tif info.Profile != \"\" {\n\t\tfmt.Fprintf(diag, \"║  Profile : %s\\n\", info.Profile)\n\t}\n\tif info.Tool != \"\" {\n\t\tfmt.Fprintf(diag, \"║  Tool    : %s\\n\", info.Tool)\n\t}\n\tif opts.Agent != \"\" {\n\t\tfmt.Fprintf(diag, \"║  Agent   : %s\\n\", opts.Agent)\n\t}\n\tif root.Job != \"\" {\n\t\tfmt.Fprintf(diag, \"║  Job     : %s\\n\", root.Job)\n\t}\n\tfmt.Fprintln(diag, \"╚══════════════════════════════════════╝\")\n\tfmt.Fprintln(diag, \"\")\n\n\t// ── Assemble the argv ───────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\t// Deliberately no --dangerously-skip-permissions (Claude) / --auto\n\t// (OpenCode): safe only inside the isolated, ephemeral container. On the\n\t// host the CLI keeps its normal per-tool confirmation prompts.\n\targv := []string{binary}\n\targv = append(argv, agentFlag...)\n\targv = append(argv, modelArgs...)\n\targv = append(argv, promptArgs...)\n\targv = append(argv, opts.Pass...)\n\n\treturn HostInvocation{\n\t\tArgv: argv,\n\t\tDir:  root.ProjectRoot,\n\t\tEnv:  hostEnv(info),\n\t}, nil\n}\n\n// hostEnv builds the child process environment for a host invocation: the\n// host's own environment plus the profile's credential keys (the KEY=VALUE\n// strings behind ProfileInfo.KeyEnv's docker -e pairs). Appended last, so\n// exec's duplicate-key handling (the last value wins) resolves every key to\n// the .env-effective value the session flow already validated — the same\n// credentials the container path forwards. OPENCODE_MODEL is excluded: it is\n// forwarded as --model instead (see BuildHostInvocation), and opencode does\n// not read it from the environment. OPENCODE_THEME is excluded too: it is a\n// container-only knob — the docker path applies it by writing a generated\n// ~/.config/opencode/tui.json inside the ephemeral container (see\n// scripts/entrypoint.sh) — and mg host runs the CLI against the user's own\n// real ~/.config/opencode, which already reflects whatever theme the user\n// configured there (the same reason mg host needs no other theme handling;\n// see tasks.md's scope note for this job). Forwarding it would be a silent,\n// untested, unused env leak.\n//\n// For OpenCode sessions, TMUX/TMUX_PANE are filtered out of the child env —\n// the same exception the docker path applies (see the terminalEnvVars comment\n// in docker.go): when OpenCode sees TMUX set it wraps its OSC 52 clipboard\n// write in tmux's DCS-passthrough escape, which default tmux configuration\n// discards entirely, so the host clipboard is never touched. Stripping TMUX\n// makes OpenCode emit plain OSC 52, which tmux's set-clipboard on handles.\n// Claude Code's host env stays the full host environment.\nfunc hostEnv(info ProfileInfo) []string {\n\tenv := os.Environ()\n\tfor i := 0; i+1 < len(info.KeyEnv); i += 2 {\n\t\tif info.KeyEnv[i] != \"-e\" {\n\t\t\tcontinue\n\t\t}\n\t\tpair := info.KeyEnv[i+1]\n\t\tif strings.HasPrefix(pair, \"OPENCODE_MODEL=\") || strings.HasPrefix(pair, \"OPENCODE_THEME=\") {\n\t\t\tcontinue\n\t\t}\n\t\tenv = append(env, pair)\n\t}\n\tif info.Tool == config.ToolOpenCode {\n\t\tvar filtered []string\n\t\tfor _, kv := range env {\n\t\t\tif strings.HasPrefix(kv, \"TMUX=\") || strings.HasPrefix(kv, \"TMUX_PANE=\") {\n\t\t\t\tcontinue\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\tfiltered = append(filtered, kv)\n\t\t}\n\t\tenv = filtered\n\t}\n\treturn env\n}\n\n// hostGlobalAgentsDir resolves the host CLI's global agents directory for a\n// tool: ~/.claude/agents/ for Claude Code, ~/.config/opencode/agents/ for\n// OpenCode (the same locations the container path mounts the global agents\n// into). The dir is derived from $HOME (os.UserHomeDir), so tests can point it\n// at a temp dir.\nfunc hostGlobalAgentsDir(tool string) string {\n\thomeDir, err := os.UserHomeDir()\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\thomeDir = os.Getenv(\"HOME\")\n\t}\n\tif tool == config.ToolOpenCode {\n\t\treturn filepath.Join(homeDir, \".config\", \"opencode\", \"agents\")\n\t}\n\treturn filepath.Join(homeDir, \".claude\", \"agents\")\n}\n\n// installHostGlobalAgents surfaces the manigot checkout's global agents\n// (<home>/agents/) to the host CLI — the host equivalent of the container\n// path's read-only mount (mg host runs the CLI directly, with no mounts).\n//\n// Delivery is per-tool, mirroring the container path's conversion split:\n//\n//   - Claude Code: each agent file is symlinked into ~/.claude/agents/ raw —\n//     the list-form frontmatter (name:/description:/tools: Read, Grep, ...)\n//     is Claude's native subagent schema. The symlinks point at the live\n//     checkout files, so edits to agents/ are reflected without re-installing.\n//   - OpenCode: OpenCode hard-errors on the list-form tools: key, so the raw\n//     file cannot be symlinked — each agent is converted first (name:/tools:\n//     stripped, permission: passed through — see convertAgentFile) and the\n//     converted copy is written into ~/.config/opencode/agents/.\n//\n// It never clobbers existing host agent config: a name already present in the\n// target dir is left untouched (the user's own host agent wins, mirroring the\n// project-overrides-global precedence). One exception: a symlink pointing at\n// the checkout's own agents/<name> is this installer's stale raw OpenCode link\n// from before the conversion fix, and is replaced with a converted copy so it\n// stops hard-erroring OpenCode. Nothing is created when the checkout has no\n// agents/ dir or no home can be located. Returns the number of agents\n// installed.\nfunc installHostGlobalAgents(tool string) (int, error) {\n\thomeDir := home.Root()\n\tif homeDir == \"\" {\n\t\treturn 0, nil\n\t}\n\tsrcDir := filepath.Join(homeDir, \"agents\")\n\tentries, err := os.ReadDir(srcDir)\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\tif os.IsNotExist(err) {\n\t\t\treturn 0, nil\n\t\t}\n\t\treturn 0, err\n\t}\n\tvar names []string\n\tfor _, e := range entries {\n\t\tif e.IsDir() || !strings.HasSuffix(e.Name(), \".md\") {\n\t\t\tcontinue\n\t\t}\n\t\tnames = append(names, e.Name())\n\t}\n\tif len(names) == 0 {\n\t\treturn 0, nil\n\t}\n\n\ttargetDir := hostGlobalAgentsDir(tool)\n\tif err := os.MkdirAll(targetDir, 0o755); err != nil {\n\t\treturn 0, err\n\t}\n\tinstalled := 0\n\tfor _, name := range names {\n\t\ttarget := filepath.Join(targetDir, name)\n\t\tif tool == config.ToolOpenCode {\n\t\t\t// OpenCode hard-errors on the list-form tools: key (see\n\t\t\t// convertAgents), so the raw checkout file cannot be symlinked:\n\t\t\t// the host target gets a converted copy instead. Never clobber an\n\t\t\t// existing host agent — except this installer's own stale raw\n\t\t\t// symlink (one pointing at the checkout's agents/<name>), which is\n\t\t\t// replaced with the converted copy.\n\t\t\tif fi, err := os.Lstat(target); err == nil {\n\t\t\t\tif fi.Mode()&os.ModeSymlink != 0 {\n\t\t\t\t\tif dest, rerr := os.Readlink(target); rerr == nil && dest == filepath.Join(srcDir, name) {\n\t\t\t\t\t\tos.Remove(target) // our stale raw link — replaced below\n\t\t\t\t\t} else {\n\t\t\t\t\t\tcontinue // a foreign symlink — the user's setup wins\n\t\t\t\t\t}\n\t\t\t\t} else {\n\t\t\t\t\tcontinue // a regular file — the user's own agent wins\n\t\t\t\t}\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\tdata, rerr := os.ReadFile(filepath.Join(srcDir, name))\n\t\t\tif rerr != nil {\n\t\t\t\tcontinue\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\tif werr := os.WriteFile(target, convertAgentFile(data), 0o644); werr != nil {\n\t\t\t\tcontinue\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\tinstalled++\n\t\t\tcontinue\n\t\t}\n\t\t// Claude Code — raw symlink (list form is Claude's native schema).\n\t\t// Never clobber an existing host agent config — the user's own file\n\t\t// (or an existing symlink, dangling or not) wins.\n\t\tif _, err := os.Lstat(target); err == nil {\n\t\t\tcontinue\n\t\t}\n\t\tif err := os.Symlink(filepath.Join(srcDir, name), target); err != nil {\n\t\t\t// Skip names we couldn't link but keep going for the rest.\n\t\t\tcontinue\n\t\t}\n\t\tinstalled++\n\t}\n\treturn installed, nil\n}\n\n// hostGlobalSkillsDir resolves the host CLI's global skills directory for a\n// tool: ~/.claude/skills/ for Claude Code, ~/.config/opencode/skills/ for\n// OpenCode (the same locations the container path mounts the global skills\n// into). The dir is derived from $HOME (os.UserHomeDir), so tests can point it\n// at a temp dir.\nfunc hostGlobalSkillsDir(tool string) string {\n\thomeDir, err := os.UserHomeDir()\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\thomeDir = os.Getenv(\"HOME\")\n\t}\n\tif tool == config.ToolOpenCode {\n\t\treturn filepath.Join(homeDir, \".config\", \"opencode\", \"skills\")\n\t}\n\treturn filepath.Join(homeDir, \".claude\", \"skills\")\n}\n\n// installHostGlobalSkills surfaces the manigot checkout's global skills\n// (<home>/skills/) to the host CLI — the host equivalent of the container\n// path's read-only mount (mg host runs the CLI directly, with no mounts).\n//\n// A skill is a directory (SKILL.md plus optional support files), so delivery\n// is per-directory, mirroring installHostGlobalAgents' per-tool split:\n//\n//   - Claude Code: each skill dir is symlinked into ~/.claude/skills/ — the\n//     symlinks point at the live checkout dirs, so edits to skills/ are\n//     reflected without re-installing.\n//   - OpenCode: each skill dir is copied into ~/.config/opencode/skills/ — a\n//     self-contained snapshot rather than a symlink, so the CLI's skills dir\n//     never points into (or writes into) the manigot checkout.\n//\n// It never clobbers existing host skill config: a name already present in the\n// target dir is left untouched (the user's own host skill wins, mirroring the\n// project-overrides-global precedence). Nothing is created when the checkout\n// has no skills/ dir or no home can be located. Returns the number of skills\n// installed.\nfunc installHostGlobalSkills(tool string) (int, error) {\n\thomeDir := home.Root()\n\tif homeDir == \"\" {\n\t\treturn 0, nil\n\t}\n\tsrcDir := filepath.Join(homeDir, \"skills\")\n\tskills, err := listSkills(srcDir)\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn 0, err\n\t}\n\tif len(skills) == 0 {\n\t\treturn 0, nil\n\t}\n\n\ttargetDir := hostGlobalSkillsDir(tool)\n\tif err := os.MkdirAll(targetDir, 0o755); err != nil {\n\t\treturn 0, err\n\t}\n\tinstalled := 0\n\tfor _, s := range skills {\n\t\ttarget := filepath.Join(targetDir, s.Name)\n\t\t// Never clobber an existing host skill — the user's own dir (or an\n\t\t// existing symlink, dangling or not) wins.\n\t\tif _, err := os.Lstat(target); err == nil {\n\t\t\tcontinue\n\t\t}\n\t\tif tool == config.ToolOpenCode {\n\t\t\t// OpenCode gets a copied snapshot (see the doc comment above).\n\t\t\tif err := copyDir(target, s.Dir); err != nil {\n\t\t\t\tcontinue // skip names we couldn't copy, keep going for the rest\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t} else {\n\t\t\t// Claude Code — raw symlink to the live checkout dir.\n\t\t\tif err := os.Symlink(s.Dir, target); err != nil {\n\t\t\t\tcontinue\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t\tinstalled++\n\t}\n\treturn installed, nil\n}\n\n// hostGlobalMetaFile resolves the host CLI's global instruction file for a\n// tool — the single file the CLI loads in every session at its *global\n// instruction* location: ~/.claude/CLAUDE.md for Claude Code (the user-global\n// memory file) and ~/.config/opencode/AGENTS.md for OpenCode (the global\n// rules file) — the same locations the container path mounts <home>/prompts/meta.md\n// into. The path is derived from $HOME (os.UserHomeDir), so tests can point it\n// at a temp dir.\nfunc hostGlobalMetaFile(tool string) string {\n\thomeDir, err := os.UserHomeDir()\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\thomeDir = os.Getenv(\"HOME\")\n\t}\n\tif tool == config.ToolOpenCode {\n\t\treturn filepath.Join(homeDir, \".config\", \"opencode\", \"AGENTS.md\")\n\t}\n\treturn filepath.Join(homeDir, \".claude\", \"CLAUDE.md\")\n}\n\n// installHostGlobalMeta surfaces the manigot checkout's system-wide meta\n// prompt (<home>/prompts/meta.md) to the host CLI — the host equivalent of the\n// container path's read-only mount (mg host runs the CLI directly, with no\n// mounts).\n//\n// It delivers a single file by COPY, never a symlink: ~/.claude/CLAUDE.md is\n// Claude Code's user-writable memory file, so a symlink would let Claude's\n// /memory writes and agent edits land back in the manigot checkout. A copied\n// file keeps the host CLI's own global instruction file fully self-contained.\n//\n// It never clobbers an existing host file: a target that already exists (the\n// user's own CLAUDE.md / AGENTS.md) wins and nothing is written. Nothing is\n// created when the checkout has no prompts/meta.md or no home can be located. Returns\n// 1 when the file was installed, 0 otherwise.\nfunc installHostGlobalMeta(tool string) (int, error) {\n\thomeDir := home.Root()\n\tif homeDir == \"\" {\n\t\treturn 0, nil\n\t}\n\tsrc := filepath.Join(homeDir, \"prompts\", \"meta.md\")\n\tif !fs.IsFile(src) {\n\t\treturn 0, nil\n\t}\n\n\ttarget := hostGlobalMetaFile(tool)\n\t// Never clobber an existing host instruction file — the user's own file\n\t// wins.\n\tif _, err := os.Lstat(target); err == nil {\n\t\treturn 0, nil\n\t}\n\tif err := os.MkdirAll(filepath.Dir(target), 0o755); err != nil {\n\t\treturn 0, err\n\t}\n\tdata, err := os.ReadFile(src)\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn 0, err\n\t}\n\tif err := os.WriteFile(target, data, 0o644); err != nil {\n\t\treturn 0, err\n\t}\n\treturn 1, nil\n}\n\n// Run executes the assembled CLI invocation, wiring stdin/stdout/stderr\n// through so Ctrl+C reaches the CLI directly (there is no container in\n// between), and returns the CLI's exit code.\nfunc (h HostInvocation) Run(stdin *os.File, stdout, stderr io.Writer) int {\n\tcmd := exec.Command(h.Argv[0], h.Argv[1:]...)\n\tcmd.Dir = h.Dir\n\tcmd.Env = h.Env\n\tcmd.Stdin = stdin\n\tcmd.Stdout = stdout\n\tcmd.Stderr = stderr\n\tif err := cmd.Run(); err != nil {\n\t\tif exitErr, ok := err.(*exec.ExitError); ok {\n\t\t\treturn exitErr.ExitCode()\n\t\t}\n\t\tfmt.Fprintf(stderr, \"mg: %v\\n\", err)\n\t\treturn 1\n\t}\n\treturn 0\n}\n","numLines":534,"startLine":1,"totalLines":534}}}
