{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01X3UrcmiDoHyCY4wdoJvqxy","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# [Project Name]\n2\t\n3\t<!--\n4\tThis is your project context, loaded by the agent at the start of every session.\n5\tmanigot is vendor-agnostic: it runs Claude Code or OpenCode against the same\n6\tproject (`mg --profile claude-pro` vs `mg --profile zai`/`--profile\n7\topencode-go`/`--profile opencode-zen`/`--profile opencode-zen-free`), and this one file serves both — manigot mounts it read-only\n8\twherever the selected tool looks for it\n9\t(/workspace/AGENTS.md for OpenCode, /workspace/.claude/CLAUDE.md for Claude\n10\tCode). Those mount paths are read-only: to change this context, edit this\n11\tfile (docs/AGENTS.md), never the mount paths.\n12\tThe same global agents (from the manigot checkout — mounted read-only into\n13\tthe container at the CLI's global agent location, or delivered into the host\n14\tCLI's config for `mg host`: symlinked for Claude Code, converted for OpenCode)\n15\tare available under @name either way, and custom\n16\tproject agents in docs/agents/ work under both tools — write them in the\n17\tbuilt-in format (name:, description:, tools: Read, Grep, ...), no per-tool\n18\tformat needed. To make a custom agent read-only under OpenCode, add a\n19\t`permission:` frontmatter block (the built-in format manigot's conversion\n20\tpasses through to OpenCode's schema — see the manigot README's agent section);\n21\tthe read-only built-in agents' blocks deny the destructive git commands\n22\t(worktree management, branch -d/-D, reset, checkout, push, ...).\n23\tGlobal skills (from the manigot checkout's `skills/` — mounted read-only into\n24\tthe container at the CLI's global skills location, or delivered into the host\n25\tCLI's config for `mg host`: symlinked dirs for Claude Code, copied dirs for\n26\tOpenCode) are loaded by the CLI at startup and are available to every agent\n27\tand to agentless invocations; custom project skills in `docs/skills/`\n28\t(`<name>/SKILL.md`, overriding global skills of the same name) work under both\n29\ttools the same way — write them in the standard format (a directory with a\n30\tSKILL.md carrying name:/description: frontmatter, plus optional support\n31\tfiles), no per-tool format needed.\n32\tA system-wide meta prompt (from the manigot checkout's `prompts/meta.md` — mounted\n33\tread-only into the container at each CLI's global instruction location\n34\t`~/.claude/CLAUDE.md` for Claude Code, `~/.config/opencode/AGENTS.md` for\n35\tOpenCode, or copied into the host CLI's own global instruction file for\n36\t`mg host`) is injected into every session above the agents, skills and this\n37\tproject context. This file still wins on conflict — it is the most\n38\tproject-specific layer.\n39\tCustom agents that must commit (like the built-in developer/reviewer/quality)\n40\tdeclare `commit: true` in their frontmatter; agents that never commit declare\n41\t`commit: false` and get a read-only git mount. The default — no agent named,\n42\tfile missing, or marker absent/unknown — is a writable git mount, so a\n43\tcommitting agent is never broken by a missing marker.\n44\tAgent files can also deny specific commands (`deny:` — a command deny-list)\n45\tand declare a network isolation level (`network:`); both are designed but not\n46\tyet enforced. `docs/agent-template.md` in the manigot checkout is a copy-me\n47\treference agent showing the full frontmatter surface and every guardrail.\n48\tEvery job-worktree session ends with a host-side sweep-commit of leftover\n49\tchanges (a `[<id>] chore: commit all` commit), so read-only agents' outputs\n50\t(like the analyst's tasks.md) are committed after their session and `mg done`"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4e2afcf5-7fc0-4a90-a6a6-e82e43a08449","uuid":"565a09a1-807f-4af4-8a15-9939241e8363","timestamp":"2026-08-31T13:46:14.930Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/workspace